2 CONT =<nowiki>---------------------------</nowiki>=
2 CONT
2 CONT From English Wikipedia:
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Conrad the Great''' (ca. 1097 – 5 February 1157) was the Margrave of Meissen from 1123 until his retirement in 1156. He was the son of Thimo, Count of Brehna, of the House of Wettin, and Ida, daughter of Otto of Nordheim. He wa
2 CONC s also Count of Wettin, Brehna, and Camburg from before 1116.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1123, he became Count of Eilenburg. That same year, Lothair of Supplinburg, Duke of Saxony, appointed him Margrave of Meissen in opposition to Wiprecht von Groitzsch, the appointee of the Emperor Henry V. Lothair also named Alb
2 CONC ert the Bear Margrave of Lusatia, while Henry named Wiprecht to that march also. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Wiprecht was unable to hold his own against his two opponents and in 1124 Conrad was securely in power in Meissen. In 1136, Lothair, then emperor, appointed him to Lusatia as well. Thereafter, Upper Lusatia remained a part of Meis
2 CONC sen and the march of Lusatia was reduced to Lower Lusatia alone.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1143, Conrad became Count of Groitzsch and Rochlitz and Vogt of Chemnitz and Naumburg. In 1147, while Conrad III of Germany was away on the Second Crusade, Conrad the Great joined Henry the Lion, Adalbert of Salzwedel, Albert t
2 CONC he Bear, and the Archbishops of Magdeburg and Bremen to organise a Crusade against the Obodrites and Wagri. In August, Conrad and Albert, with the bishops of Magdeburg, Havelburg, and Brandenburg, massed their forces at Magdeburg.
2 CONC  The Obodrite prince Niklot and his fortressess of Dubin and Dimin were besieged. Both he and Pribislav, another Obodrite prince, were forced to accept Christianity and make peace.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the following years, he founded the monastery of St Peter auf dem Lauterberg (Petersberg), to which he retired on 30 November 1156. He died and was buried there next to his wife, Luitgard (Lucarda) von Elchingen-Ravenstein, dau
2 CONC ghter of Adalbert, Count of Elchingen-Ravenstein and Bertha of Hohenstaufen, daughter of Frederick I, Duke of Swabia and Agnes of Germany.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage and issue
2 CONT
2 CONT Luitgard of Elchingen-Ravenstein, whom he had married before 1119, had blessed him with many children. His eldest surviving son, Otto II, Margrave of Meissen, succeeded him in Meissen, while his second surviving son, Dietrich I, s
2 CONC ucceeded in Lusatia. His son Henry I, Count of Wettin married Sophia of Sommerschenburg, Countess Palatine of Saxony, daughter of Count Fredrick II of Sommerschenburg and Countess Liutgard of Stade, queen dowager of Denmark.
2 CONT
2 CONT *1. Heinrich (i.e. Henry; died young)
2 CONT *2. Otto II
2 CONT *3. Dietrich I
2 CONT *4. Dedo V. der Feiste
2 CONT *5. Henry I, Count of Wettin, married (1) Sophia of Sommerschenburg (d. 1189 or 1190), daughter of Count Fredrick II of Sommerschenburg, Count Palatine of Saxony and his wife Countess Liutgard of Stade (later queen of Denmark).
2 CONT *6. Friedrich I von Brehna
2 CONT *7. Oda
2 CONT *8. Adela, Queen consort of Denmark
2 CONT *9. Bertha
2 CONT *10. Sophie
2 CONT *11. Gertrud
2 CONT *12. Agnes II, Princess-Abbess of Quedlinburg
2 CONT
2 CONT References
2 CONT
2 CONT Thompson, James Westfall. Feudal Germany, Volume II. New York: Frederick Ungar Publishing Co., 1928.
2 CONT
