1 OCCU Dame, de Constantinople, Queen of Sicily, Princesa de Byzancio, principessa consorte di Sicilia (1192-1193) e poi duchessa consorte di Svevia (dal 1197) e infine regina consorte di Germania (dal 1198) sino al 1208
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Irene Angelina
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Irene_Angelina
2 CONT
2 CONT Irene Angelina (1181 – 1208) was the daughter of the Byzantine Emperor Isaac II Angelos by his first wife Herina Tornikaina. Her paternal grandparents were Andronikos Dukas Angelos and Euphrosyne Kastamonitissa.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1193 she married Roger III of Sicily, but he died on 24 December 1193. Irene was captured in the German invasion of Sicily on 29 December 1194 and was married on 25 May 1197 to Philip of Swabia. In Germany, she was renamed Mari
2 CONC a.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her father, who had been deposed in 1195, urged her to get Philip's support for his reinstatement; her brother, Alexius, subsequently spent some time at Philip's court during the preparations for the Fourth Crusade. She thus had a
2 CONC n early influence on the eventual diversion of the Crusade to Constantinople in 1204.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was described by Walther von der Vogelweide as "the rose without a thorn, the dove without guile".
2 CONT
2 CONT Philip and Irene had four daughters:
2 CONT
2 CONT #Beatrice of Hohenstaufen (1198-1212), married Otto IV, Holy Roman Emperor, died without issue.
2 CONT
2 CONT #Cunigunde of Hohenstaufen (1200-1248), married King Wenceslaus I, King of Bohemia, by whom she had issue.
2 CONT
2 CONT #Marie of Hohenstaufen (3 April 1201- 29 March 1235), married Henry II, Duke of Brabant, by whom she had issue.
2 CONT
2 CONT #Elisabeth of Hohenstaufen (1203-1235), married King Ferdinand III of Castile, by whom she had issue.
2 CONT
2 CONT and two sons (called Reinald and Frederick) who died in infancy.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the murder of her husband (21 April 1208), Irene - who was pregnant by that time - retired to the Burg Hohenstaufen. There, four months later (27 August 1208), she gave birth to a daughter (called Beatrice Postuma); but both
2 CONC  mother and child died shortly afterwards. She was buried in the family mausoleum in the Staufen proprietary monastery of Lorch Abbey, along with her daughter and sons. Her grave, now destroyed, cannot be reconstructed today.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Irene Angelina
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia 
2 CONT
2 CONT Irene Angelina (1181 - 1208) was the daughter of the Byzantine emperor Isaac II Angelos by his first wife Herina.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1193 she married Roger III of Sicily, but he died on 24 December 1193. Irene was captured in the German invasion of Sicily on 29 December 1194 and was married on 25 May 1197 to Philip of Swabia. In Germany, she was renamed Mari
2 CONC a.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her father, who had been deposed in 1195, urged her to get Philip's support for his reinstatement; her brother, Alexius, subsequently spent some time at Philip's court during the preparations for the Fourth Crusade. She thus had a
2 CONC n early influence on the eventual diversion of the Crusade to Constantinople in 1204.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was described by Walther von der Vogelweide as "the rose without a thorn, the dove without guile."
2 CONT
2 CONT Philip and Irene had four daughters:
2 CONT
2 CONT Beatrice of Hohenstaufen (1198-1212), married Otto IV, Holy Roman Emperor
2 CONT
2 CONT Cunigunde of Hohenstaufen (1200-1248), married King Wenceslaus I, King of Bohemia
2 CONT
2 CONT Maria of Hohenstaufen (1201-1235), married Henry II, Duke of Brabant
2 CONT
2 CONT Elisabeth of Hohenstaufen (1203-1235), married King Ferdinand III of Castile
2 CONT
2 CONT and two other children, both sons -Reinald and an unnamed son, possibly called Frederick- who died in infancy.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the murder of her husband (21 April 1208), Irene -who was pregnant by that time- retired to the Burg Hohenstaufen. There, four months later (27 August 1208), she gave birth to a daughter (called Beatrice Postuma); but both m
2 CONC other and child died shortly afterwards. She was buried in the Staufen Mausoleum in the Monastery of Lorch, along with her daughter and sons. Her grave, now destroyed, cannot be reconstructed today.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources
2 CONT
2 CONT O city of Byzantium: annals of Niketas Choniates tr. Harry J. Magoulias (Detroit: Wayne State University Press, 1984).
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Irene_Angelina
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
