1 NOTE {geni:about_me} =Heinrich IV der Jüngere zu Stolberg=
2 CONT
2 CONT * Son of Otto I Graf van Stolberg and Sofie Honstein, Countess zu Wernigerode
2 CONT * Heinrich VI (ruled 1347–1368), Bishop of Merseburg (1384–1393)
2 CONT
2 CONT =Married=
2 CONT
2 CONT * Married: '''Elisabeth née Countess of Hohenstein von Mansfeld,'''. Heinrich XVI, Count of Stolberg, son of Otto, Count of Stolberg and (Miss) von Honstein; There were at least 2 other children (alive in 1391) born to this marria
2 CONC ge. Elisabeth Was born at of Mansfeld, Sachsen, Prussia. She died after 6 March 1398.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Children=
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Otto zu Stolberg,'''  d. a 1394
2 CONT * '''Albrecht zu Stolberg''' d. a 1400
2 CONT * '''Bodo VII, Count of Stolberg-Wernigerode.''' d. 15 Mar 1455, after the death of his father, he first ruled jointly with his elder brother Henry, who died around 1416. For the first time in 1403 Botho entered independently acti
2 CONC ng in appearance was acquired as the fiefdom Rosperwenda.
2 CONT * '''Sophie zu Stolberg,'''  b. 1372, d. 22 Jan 1463
2 CONT * '''Anna von Stolberg,''' + b. c 1377, d. 1436
2 CONT * '''Heinrich zu Stolberg,''' b. 1379, d. a 28 Jun 1416
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://www.wikiwand.com/en/County_of_Wernigerode House of Stolberg]
2 CONT
2 CONT Count Henry of Wernigerode was the last male representative of the dynasty. He demanded from Archbishop Günther II of Magdeburg the enfeoffment of the castle and town of Wernigerode for himself and his two Stolberg cousins Henry a
2 CONC nd his brother Bodo the Elder, then ruling over the Thuringian County of Stolberg south of the Harz range. The inheritance was secured on 30 June 1414. As one of the heirs chosen by the last Wernigerode count, Henry of Stolberg, d
2 CONC ied young, Count Henry of Wernigerode in 1417 had the people of the county swear fealty to Count Bodo of Stolberg as the future owner of the lordship of Wernigerode.
2 CONT
2 CONT As Count Bodo had the fortune to become the sole Stolberg heir at that same time, it became necessary, however, for him to establish his permanent seat of power at Stolberg Castle. This was a setback for the further development of
2 CONC  Wernigerode because, after the death of Count Henry of Wernigerode in 1429, no count resided permanently in the town. The demise of Wernigerode was reinforced by the fact that Count Bodo started in 1438, to pledge the castle and 
2 CONC its associated lordship.
2 CONT
2 CONT It was a very lucrative fief that included the spiritual fiefdom of the Monastery of St. George and St. Sylvester at Wernigerode, the abbeys of Himmelpforten, Ilsenburg and Drübeck, and the villages of Drübeck, Reddeber, Langeln w
2 CONC ith its Teutonic Order, Wasserleben with its nunnery, and Veckenstedt with its important aristocratic seat (Adelshof). The lordship of Wernigerode was thus much more significant than that of the stem county of Stolberg which did n
2 CONC ot have a single monastery within its borders.
2 CONT
2 CONT The actual county reduced in size during the 16th century to the comital district (gräfliche Amt) of Wernigerode that, for example, had the following tax receipts (Schoss) in 1543/44:
2 CONT
2 CONT * Wernigerode Altstadt - 100 marks
2 CONT * Wernigerode Neustadt - 13 marks, 16 pieces
2 CONT * Drübeck - 30.5 marks
2 CONT * Wasserleben - 21.5 marks
2 CONT * Langeln - 22 marks
2 CONT * Silstedt - 5 marks
2 CONT * Ilsenburg - 2 marks
2 CONT * Darlingerode - 3 marks
2 CONT * For the abandoned village of Steinbruch near Drübeck - 4 Vierdung
2 CONT * Inheritance tax from Wernigerode, Veckenstedt, Silstedt, Langeln, Wasserleben and the chapter of St. Sylvester and George at Wernigerode etc.
2 CONT
2 CONT In toto the Amtmann of Wernigerode collected 5,120 guilders in 1543/44, compared with just 4,247 guelders the year before. Against that the expenditure for the district was 3,456 guelders, leaving a profit of 1,664 guelders. That 
2 CONC was a considerable sum, if one considers that in that year a new minting workshop had been built for 50 guelders.
2 CONT
2 CONT For more than 200 years, both territories were ruled in personal union by the House of Stolberg. Held by the Stolberg-Stolberg line from 1548, the comital dynasty again divided their dominions when in 1710 Count Christian Ernest r
2 CONC eceived the Stolberg-Wernigerode territory from the estate of his uncle Count Ernest of Stolberg. He relocated his residence to Wernigerode Castle which he had extensively restored. Nevertheless, in 1714 he had to acknowledge the 
2 CONC overlordship of the Prussian Principality of Halberstadt. Finally mediatized, the county was incorporated into the Prussian Province of Saxony in 1815.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Sources=
2 CONT
2 CONT * Köbler, Gerhard (1988). Historisches Lexikon der deutschen Länder (in German). Munich: Verlag C. H. Beck. p. 639. ISBN 3-406-33290-0.
2 CONT * Schwineköper, Berent (1987). Handbuch der historischen Stätten Deutschlands, Band 11, Provinz Sachsen/Anhalt (in German). Stuttgart: Alfred Kröner Verlag. p. 644. ISBN 3-520-31402-9.
2 CONT * Christian Friedrich Kesslin: Nachrichten von Schriftstellern und Künstlern der Grafschaft Wernigerode vom Jahre 1074 bis 1855. Commissions-Verlag von Gebrüder Bänsch in Magdeburg 1856. XII, 312 pp.
2 CONT * Jan Habermann: Die Grafen von Wernigerode. Herrschaftsprofil, Wirkungsbereich und Königsnähe hochadliger Potentaten am Nordharz im späten Mittelalter. Norderstedt 2008 - ISBN 978-3-8370-2820-1
2 CONT * Jan Habermann: Die Herrschaftsausweitung der Grafen von Wernigerode am Nordharz (1249 - 1369) digitalised
2 CONT * House of Stolberg - https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/House_of_Stolberg
2 CONT * Unknown author, Journal of Anciety and Medieval Studies, Vol. VI, p. 23/24.
2 CONT * Europaische Stammtafeln, by Wilhelm Karl, Prinz zu Isenburg, Vol. XVII, Tafel 99.
2 CONT * Europaische Stammtafeln, by Wilhelm Karl, Prinz zu Isenburg, Vol. XIX, Tafel 85.
2 CONT * Detlev Schwennicke, Europaische Stammtafeln, New Series, Vol. XVII, Tafel 99.
2 CONT * Stolberg von, Family - http://gallusmagnus.nl/index.php/Stolberg_von,_Familie
