1 NAME Konrad II /Ritter von Wanebergen/ the Younger
2 GIVN Konrad II
2 NSFX the Younger
2 _MARNM Ritter von Wanebergen
1 NAME Kuno II/Cuno II/Cord II von Wahneberge(n)/Wanenbergen/Wanenberge/Waneberge or de Wane(n)berch
1 SEX M
1 OCCU Edelherr
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Niedersachsen Nobleman, Knight/Miles, Ministerialis der Welfen (to Duke/Herzog [Pfalzgraf] Heinrich V, von Braunschweig -- The eldest son of Heinrich der Löwen), Landowner, and the disputed "heir" (last Schirmvogt)
2 CONC  to the Vogtei of Verden an der Aller.
2 CONT
2 CONT Kuno II was the son of the Guelphic Ministerialis, Kuno I (alias: ''Conradus Advocatus Verdensis'' or ''Conradi Advocati Verdensis''). 
2 CONT
2 CONT Kuno the Elder (born pre-1162) is both the first documented von Wanebergen scion and ''Voigt zu Verden'' from the ''Adelsgeschlecht'' von Wa(h)nebergen/Wane(n)berg(en). He was first mentioned in the Urkundenbücher in 1162 as havin
2 CONC g captured the attention of the Duke, Henry the Lion (Heinrich der Löwen, von Welf/Guelph), resulting in his appointment as a Guelphic Ministerialis and member of Henry's Ducal entourage. The ''Adelsgeschlecht'' von Wanebergen's p
2 CONC re-1162 origins are unclear, but the family is argued to have been a venerable ''Dynastae'' accorded the medieval status of "Viri Nobiles" (in which the document accords them a nobiliary status similar to that of the extinct Lords
2 CONC  von Westen of ''Amt'' Westen) and were even cited as ''Edelherren'' in certain sources (particularly in revisionist Medieval scholarship).
2 CONT
2 CONT Some sources assert that the ''Adelsgeschlecht'' von Wanebergen could have been ''edelfrei'' that were either forced by dire circumstances to subject themselves to vassalage (under the most powerful family in ''Niedersachsen'') or
2 CONC  prompted by tactical ambition to join the quickly rising rank of elevated ''ministeriales'' during the High Middle Ages. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The family owned numerous farming villages in ''Amt'' Westen (prior to confiscation/expropriation and forced liquidation of land holdings in and around Verden by both papal and imperial decrees and unrelenting ecclesiastical press
2 CONC ure applied by Prince-Bishop Iso/Ÿso [Count/Graf] von Wölpe of Verden), parts of Mecklenburg, and later in Saxony (possessions in the Sorbian village of Mikow/Mücka). The von Wanebergen lands are believed to have included the foll
2 CONC owing villages/municipalities/districts: 1) Verden (a Guelph fiefdom), 2) Wahnebergen village (bauerndorf), 3) Mölme/Moule/Mauloh (extinct by/in 1610), 4) Stederdorf/Stederthorpe, 5) Sibudeswerthere, 6) the Burgfeldes (now: the Bu
2 CONC rgstelle[s] on Verden's outskirts) where Verden's Alte(n) Burg stood, 7) Altenwerder/Oldenwerdere, 8) 3/4s of Sarmstorf, 9) possessions in Mikow/Mücka, Saxony, and 10) possibly possessions in Güstrow, Malchin, and Schwerin -- wher
2 CONC e the family had been active and held political offices into the late 1400s. The last recorded marriage of the Mecklenburg branch was between Elisabeth von Wanenberg(h) and Levin von Gamm of Alt-Schwerin circa 1472/1475.
