2 FORM jpg
2 _PRIM Y
1 NOTE Adelheid of Meissen (Czech: Adléta Míšenská, c. 1160 - 2 February 1211) was the first Queen consort of Ottokar I of Bohemia.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage
2 CONT Adelheid was born about 1160 as the daughter of Otto II, Margrave of Meissen. She met her future husband in the 1170s, in the time of his exile. It is possible that this marriage (1178) was forced on grounds of her pregnancy. Adel
2 CONC heid gave birth to a son, Vratislaus, soon.
2 CONT
2 CONT Shortly after the marriage the couple could return to Bohemia-Ottokar's brother Frederick assumed the reins of Bohemia and Ottokar was one of the leaders of his army. In 1192 Ottokar himself became duke but in 1193 was deposed. He
2 CONC  left Bohemia together with his family then.
2 CONT
2 CONT Adelheid came to her brother and Premysl Ottokar became a mercenary of German dukes. In this time the couple got estranged. Ottokar decided to solve the problem in a way exclusive to all dynastic principles.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Two Queens
2 CONT In the end of 1197 Ottokar became duke for the second time. He repudiated his wife and also his adult son. Ottokar was at least 40 at that time and risked losing the heir. Adelheid and her daughters stayed in Meissen, while her so
2 CONC n Vratislaus became a mercenary in Germany and Italy.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1199, Ottokar (now the king) divorced Adelheid, on grounds of consanguinity. He married Constance of Hungary later in the same year.[2] Adelheid did not waive her rights. In 1205 she returned to Prague for a while. Ottokar deci
2 CONC ded to marry his daughter with Adelheid, Dagmar/Markéta, to Valdemar II of Denmark in this time. However, Constance gave birth to a son, later king Wenceslaus, in 1205. Then Adelheid and her daughters left Bohemia permanently.
2 CONT
2 CONT The pope decided that the cessation of marriage is legal. Adelheid still struggled, but in 1210 definitely lost. She died in 1211 in Meissen.
2 CONT
2 CONT Issue
2 CONT Vratislav (before 1181 - after 1225) 
2 CONT Dagmar (Markéta) (ca 1186 - May 24, 1213), married to Valdemar II of Denmark, King of Denmark 
2 CONT Bo?islava 
2 CONT Hedvika 
2 CONT
2 CONT References
2 CONT CORNEJ, P. a kol. 234 ceských osobností. Praha : Fragment, 2004. 
2 CONT ?arochová G. V. - Radostný údel vdovský. Královny-vdovy premyslovských Cech. Praha, 2004. 
2 CONT
2 CONT References
2 CONT ^ Profile of Ottokar I , his wives and children in "Medieval Lands" by Charles Cawley 
2 CONT ^ Profile of Ottokar I , his wives and children in "Medieval Lands" by Charles Cawley 
