1 NOTE {geni:about_me} *https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Daniel_of_Moscow
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Daniil Aleksandrovich''' (Russian: Даниил Александрович; 1261 – 4 March 1303) was the youngest son of Alexander Nevsky and forefather of all the Grand Dukes of Moscow.
2 CONT
2 CONT  Early life
2 CONT
2 CONT Prince Daniel of Moscow was born at Vladimir, capital of the Great Vladimir-Suzdal principality, in 1261. He was the fourth and youngest son of Saint Prince Alexander Nevsky—famous in the history of the Russian State and the Russi
2 CONC an Orthodox Church — and his second wife, Princess Vassa. [1] One of the most junior princes in the House of Rurik, Daniel is thought to have been named after his celebrated relative, Daniel of Galicia.
2 CONT
2 CONT  Government
2 CONT
2 CONT His father died when he was only two years old. Of his father's patrimonies, he received the least valuable, Moscow.[1] When he was a child, the tiny principality was being governed by tiuns (deputies), appointed by his paternal u
2 CONC ncle, Grand Prince Yaroslav III.
2 CONT
2 CONT Daniel has been credited with founding the first Moscow monasteries, namely the Lord's Epiphany, and The Danilov Monestery (Saint Daniel Monestery): named in his honor, situated on the right bank of the Moskva River at a distance 
2 CONC of 5 miles from the Moscow Kremlin, and founded, by Daniel, as the first monastery wooden church of St. Daniel-Stylite no later than 1282.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT He also built the first stone church in the Moscow Kremlin in the 1280s, dedicated to the Great Martyr Demetrius.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Daniel took part in his brothers'—Dmitri of Pereslavl and Andrey of Gorodets—struggle for the right to govern Vladimir and Novgorod, respectively. After Dmitry's death in 1294, Daniel made an alliance with Mikhail of Tver and Ivan
2 CONC  of Pereslavl against Andrey of Gorodets of Novgorod.
2 CONT
2 CONT Daniel's participation in the struggle for Novgorod in 1296 indicated Moscow's increasing political influence. Constantine, the prince of Ryazan, tried to capture the Moscow lands with the help of a Mongol force. Prince Daniel def
2 CONC eated it near Pereslavl. This was a first victory over the Tatars, though not a tremendous victory, but it was noteworthy as a first push towards freedom.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1300, he went to Ryazan with an army and imprisoned the ruler of the Ryazan Principality "by some ruse", as the chronicle says, and destroyed a multitude of Tatars. To secure his release, the prisoner ceded to Daniel his fortre
2 CONC ss of Kolomna. It was an important acquisition, as now Daniel controlled all the length of the Moskva River.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1302 his childless nephew and ally, Ivan of Pereslavl, bequeathed to Daniel all his lands, including Pereslavl-Zalessky.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT During the Mongol occupation and internecine wars among the Rus' princes, Daniel created peace in Moscow without bloodshed. During 30 years of ruling Daniel participated in battles only once.[1] According to legend, Daniel was pop
2 CONC ular and respected by his subjects for his meekness, humility and peacefulness.
2 CONT
2 CONT  Death and canonization
2 CONT
2 CONT At the age of 42 on the 5th of March in 1303 St. Daniel died. Before his death he became a monk and, according to his will, was buried in the cemetery of the St. Daniel Monastery. This was a common cemetery and by being buried the
2 CONC re he was demonstrating humility. He was canonized by the Russian Orthodox Church in 1791 but only for local veneration. [2]
2 CONT
2 CONT In the year 1330, the monastery was moved to Kremlin and the monastery was turned into a common parish. The cemetery became open to the public and his grave was lost. On August 30, 1652, many relics from Daniel of Moscow were foun
2 CONC d and were placed in a tomb in the Danilov Monastery in his honor.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT  Marriage and children
2 CONT
2 CONT His wife was named Maria. They had at least six children:
2 CONT
2 CONT *Yury of Moscow (1281 – 21 September 1325).
2 CONT *Aleksandr Daniilovich (died Autumn 1308).
2 CONT *Boris Daniilovich, Prince of Kostroma (died 1320).
2 CONT *Afanasy Daniilovich, Prince of Novgorod (died 1322).
2 CONT *Fedora Daniilovna. Married Yaroslav Romanovich, Prince of Ryazan (died 1299).
2 CONT *Ivan I of Moscow (1288 – 31 March 1340).
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------
2 CONT [http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/RUSSIA,%20Rurik.htm#Daniildied1303B Medlands]
2 CONT
2 CONT DANIIL Aleksandrovich, son of ALEKSANDR Iaroslavich "Nievskiy" Grand Prince of Vladimir & his first wife Paraskeviya [Aleksandra] Bryacheslavna of Polotsk (1261-4/5 Mar 1303).  Prince of Novgorod 1255, 1264-1272.  Prince of Moscow
2 CONC  [1283].  He supported his brother Dmitry in his dispute with their brother Andrei, refusing to visit Sarai to renew his allegiance to the Mongol overlords[990].  On the death of his nephew Ivan Dmitrievich Prince of Pereyaslavl Z
2 CONC alesskii, Daniil prevented his brother Andrei from retaking the principality and assumed control of it himself. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''m''' MARIA, daughter of ---[991].  
2 CONT
