2 CONT 1.         '''KONSTANTINOS Tornikes''' (-killed in Bulgaria [1205/06]).  Adontz quotes a letter from Mikhael Akominate addressed to Demetrios Tornikes which names his father Konstantinos and his grandfather Demetrios[875].  A seal
2 CONC  dated to [1190] names "Konstantinos Tornikes, sebastos"[876].  The History of Niketas Choniates names "præfectus urbis Constantinus Tornicius" as head of the imperial guard which suppressed a riot in Constantinople in 1201[877]. 
2 CONC  Konstantinos Tornikes, uncle ("θείου") of Emperor Alexios IV, is named at Patmos, dated to 1203[878].  The History of Niketas Choniates records that "Dromi logothetam Constantinum Tornicem" was captured by the Bulgarians after th
2 CONC e battle of Adrianople and later killed[879].  
2 CONT
2 CONT '''m''' [--- Komnene], [illegitimate daughter or granddaughter of Emperor MANUEL I]  (-after 1201).  Her supposed origin is deduced from a single primary source as follows.  The eulogy written by Euthymios Tornikes for his father 
2 CONC Demetrios, dated 1201, refers to "λογοθέτου γηνη… σου νυμφη" ("the wife of the logothetes, his daughter-in-law") and adds that the lady in question was "η εχ της πρώτης χαι Βασιλιχης των Κομνηνων χρυσέας σειρας η ανδρεια χαι σωφρω
2 CONC ν" ("descended from the first and imperial series of gold of the Komnenos, brave and wise")[880].  It is highly probable that the text refers to the wife of Konstantinos Tornikes, who is referred to as "dromi logothetam Constantin
2 CONC um Tornicem" by Niketas Choniates (see above)[881].  Varzos interprets the phrase "imperial series of gold of the Komnenos" as meaning that the wife of Konstantinos Tornikes was "one who emanated from Emperors Alexios, Ioannes and
2 CONC  Manuel Komnenos", in other words from the direct line of the three Komnenos emperors[882].  If this interpretation is correct, descent from any one or two of these emperors would not constitute a "series", nor would descent from 
2 CONC Emperor Andronikos I who counted only Emperor Alexios among his direct ancestors.  This would necessarily mean that the lady in question was the daughter or granddaughter (the chronology would support either case) of Emperor Manue
2 CONC l I, evidently illegitimate if she was his daughter.  However, it is not known whether such a poetic turn of phrase in this type of eulogy should be interpreted so literally.  It should also be noted that Varzos´s interpretation d
2 CONC oes not appear to consign a meaning to the word "πρώτης" ("first").]  
2 CONT
2 CONT    Konstantinos & his wife had one child: 
2 CONT *a)         DEMETRIOS Tornikes Komnenos (-[1252]).  Adontz quotes a letter from Mikhael Akominate addressed to Demetrios Tornikes which names his father Konstantinos and his grandfather Demetrios[883].  Georgios Akropolites names 
2 CONC "Demetrios Tornices, qui summam rerum publicarum administrabat…" among the important nobles at the court of Emperor Ioannes Batatzes[884].  -        see below. 
