1 NOTE {geni:about_me} There are three different versions of who Giedymin's - Gediminas' father, grandfather and great-grandfather are: 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Polish-Lithuanian version: Pucuwerus Rex Lethowie, Trojden and Wołk. 
2 CONT 2. Samogitian version: Butvydas - Pucuwerus, Dausprungas - Dowsprunk and Ringaudas - Ryngold. 
2 CONT 3. Russian version: Витень - Witeń, Пройденъ - Projden and Видъ - Wid. 
2 CONT
2 CONT And these three versions are contradictory and irreconcilable, and this controversy will continue forever. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ---------- 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Giedymin''', also Gedymin, lit. Gediminas, bel. Hiedymin (born around 1275 [1], died in December 1341 near Wielona) - the Grand Duke of Lithuania in the years 1316–1341, the founder of the Giedyminowicz dynasty, Jagiełło's gran
2 CONC dfather. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Origin
2 CONT
2 CONT Gediminas' origin raises some doubts in the literature. Usually he is considered the son of Butywid (Pukuwer) [2]. The Lithuanian historian Edvardas Gudavičius draws attention to the fact that at the end of the 13th century Lithua
2 CONC nia was ruled by a pair of rulers (eg Budikid and Budiwid); For this reason, it is possible that Gediminas might have been a nephew and not a son of Pukuwer.
2 CONT
2 CONT Control
2 CONT
2 CONT Gediminas took power after the death of his brother, Witenes. Throughout his reign, he struggled with the pressure from the Teutonic state. At the same time, taking advantage of the Golden Horde crisis, he managed to unite very la
2 CONC rge areas south-east of Lithuania under his rule, thus enlarging the territory of his country twice.
2 CONT
2 CONT Around 1318 (through the marriage of his son Olgierd) he took control of Witebsk, and in the following years also Pińsk, Wołyń, and Podlasie (through the marriage of his son Lubart). Around 1330 he captured Kiev, which, together w
2 CONC ith the Duchy of Kiev, handed over to his brother Fedor [3] (the possible dependence of Fedor on the Golden Horde remains a controversial issue). Gediminas rule extended to the east as far as the land of Smolensk.
2 CONT
2 CONT He maintained proper contacts with Riga (mainly commercial). In 1323, at the request of his son-in-law, Prince Wańka of Płock, he invaded the Duchy of Dobrzyń in order to resolve by force a border dispute with Duchess Anastasia [4
2 CONC ]. Announcing that he wants to accept Christianity, in 1324 he concluded a four-year truce with the Teutonic Order [3]. In 1325, he entered into an alliance with Poland (his daughter, Aldona, married in the same year the heir to t
2 CONC he Polish throne - Kazimierz, later called the Great). As a consequence of the alliance, in the spring of 1326 Gediminas troops took part in an invasion of Brandenburg together with the Polish ruler Władysław Łokietek [5]. In 1337
2 CONC  he repelled the Teutonic invasion [3]. He had good relations with the papacy, which he promised on several occasions to be baptized. He also undertook successful efforts to create a separate, Orthodox Lithuanian metropolis.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gediminas is often referred to as the creator of the Lithuanian superpower. His conquests had a huge impact on the history of Ruthenian lands, as well as on the Lithuanian state, a large part of which was quickly shed.
2 CONT
2 CONT Division of the state
2 CONT
2 CONT With the death of Gediminas, the Lithuanian state was divided between the seven sons of the prince: Monwid, Kiejstut, Olgierd, Narymunt, Koriat, Lubart and Jawnuta (whom he appointed as the ruler of Vilnius and his successor).
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage and offspring
2 CONT
2 CONT Not much information has survived about Gediminas' marriage. The chronicle of Bychowiec from the 16th century states that his wife was Jewna. Currently, in historical literature, she is considered the only wife of this prince and 
2 CONC the mother of all his children [1]. Gediminas had eight sons and six daughters. The order of the children - especially the sons - remains debatable.
2 CONT
2 CONT The sons of Gediminas were:
2 CONT
2 CONT * Narymunt (c. 1300 - February 2, 1348), prince of Pinsk, Połack and Nowogródek
2 CONT * Witold Giedyminowic (c. 1303–1336), prince of Trotsky,
2 CONT * Olgierd (c. 1304, died 1377), Grand Duke of Lithuania from 1345,
2 CONT * Koriat (Michał) (c. 1306 - c. 1365), the prince of Nowogródek,
2 CONT * Jawnuta (approx. 1306/1309 - after 1366), Grand Duke of Lithuania 1341–1344, Duke of Zasław 1346–1366,
2 CONT * Kiejstut (Kestutis) (approx. 1308/1310 - August 15, 1382), prince of Trotsky, Grand Duke of Lithuania 1381–1382,
2 CONT * Lubart (Lubko, Lubartas, Dymitr; c. 1312 / 1315–1384), the Duke of Połock, Włodzimierz, Łuck from 1340, Wołyński 1340–1349, 1350–1366, 1371–1383, Duke of Halych 1340–1349.
2 CONT * Monwid (ca. 1313 / 1315–1348), the Duke of Kierno and Słonim.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gediminas' daughters were:
2 CONT
2 CONT * Elżbieta (approx. 1302–1364), from 1316 the wife of Wacław, the Duke of Płock,
2 CONT * Maria (approx. 1304 / 1307–1349), from 1320 the wife of Dmitri the Groźnooki, Grand Duke of Tver,
2 CONT * Aldona Anna (approx. 1311/1313 - May 25, 1339), from 1325 the wife of Casimir III the Great,
2 CONT * NN (Eufemia?) (Ca.1314/1317 - January / February 1341) - from 1331 wife of Bolesław Jerzy II,
2 CONT * NN (Helena?) (Ca.1315/1319 - probably after June 23, 1339), wife of Andrzej Kozielski,
2 CONT * Augusta Anastazja (ca. 1316/1321 - March 11, 1345), wife of Semyon Dumny, Grand Duke of Moscow.
2 CONT
2 CONT Footnotes
2 CONT
2 CONT  J. Tęgowski, The first generations of Giedyminowicz, Poznań - Wrocław 1999, p. 15.
2 CONT  For example, M. Duczmal, The Jagiellonians, pp. 14–15.
2 CONT  Giedymin - PWN Encyclopedia - a source of credible and reliable knowledge, encyklopedia.pwn.pl [accessed 2020-12-28] (pol.).
2 CONT  The History of Mazovia, Volume I, Henryk Samsonowicz (ed.), Pułtusk 2006, p. 238
2 CONT  Władysław I Łokietek - PWN Encyclopedia - a source of credible and reliable knowledge, encyklopedia.pwn.pl [accessed 2020-12-28] (pol.). 
