1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Fiodor''' of Kiev (Lithuanian: Teodoras) (the 14th century), was the prince of Kiev (Kyiv). Most likely he was the son of Pucuwerus Rex Lethowie, and a younger brother of Giedymin, Grand Duke of Lithuania. Only 
2 CONC a couple of short notes survive regarding Fiodor's life. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In early 1320s[1] Gediminas won the Battle on the Irpen' River against Stanislav of Kiev and captured the city.[2] The Tatars, who also claimed Kiev, retaliated in 1324–1325. The Lithuanian Chronicles mention that Gediminas instal
2 CONC led his deputy Algimantas, son of Mindaugas from Olshanski family. There were some attempts to claim that Algimantas was Fiodor's pagan name, but they are discharged by evidence that Algimantas was baptized as Mikhail.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1331 Vasily Kalika, a newly consecrated Archbishop of Novgorod, was traveling from Volodymyr-Volynskyi to Veliky Novgorod.[4] On his way he was stopped by Fiodor, Duke of Kiev, a Tatar tax collector (basqaq), and fifty men.[5] 
2 CONC The presence of a Tatar official led historians to believe that while Kiev was ruled by a Lithuanian, it had to pay a tribute to the Golden Horde.[6] Lithuanians gained full control of the city after the victorious Battle of Blue 
2 CONC Waters in 1362.[7] According to the Gustynskaya Chronicle, after the battle Fiodor was replaced as Duke of Kiev by Vladimir, son of Algirdas.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT For a long time scholars assumed that Fiodor was of Rurikid origin (descendant of Oleg I of Chernigov) because of his Christian name. However, in 1916 Russian historian Mikhail Priselkov published a list of property belonging to T
2 CONC heognostus, Metropolitan of Moscow.[8] The list, compiled in 1331, listed two silver cups given to Theognostus by Fiodor, brother of Gediminas.[5] Modern historians agree that Fiodor from the list and Fiodor from Kiev was one and 
2 CONC the same person. No other evidence survives regarding Fiodor's family.
2 CONT
2 CONT See also
2 CONT Family of Gediminas
2 CONT References
2 CONT  Historians disagree on exact dating: Maciej Stryjkowski provided 1320/21, Aleksandr I. Rogov argues for 1322, C. S. Rowell for 1323, Feliks Shabul'do for 1324, Romas Batūra for 1325.
2 CONT  Rowell, C. S. (1994). Lithuania Ascending: A Pagan Empire Within East-Central Europe, 1295-1345. Cambridge Studies in Medieval Life and Thought: Fourth Series. Cambridge University Press. p. 97. ISBN 978-0-521-45011-9.
2 CONT  Rowell, C. S. Lithuania Ascending, p. 104.
2 CONT  Rowell, C. S. Lithuania Ascending, p. 176–177.
2 CONT  Rowell, C. S. Lithuania Ascending, p. 100.
2 CONT  Simas Sužiedėlis, ed. (1970–1978). "Theodore". Encyclopedia Lituanica. V. Boston, Massachusetts: Juozas Kapočius. pp. 446–447. LCC 74-114275.
2 CONT  Gudavičius, Edvardas (2004). "Teodoras". In Vytautas Spečiūnas (ed.). Lietuvos valdovai (XIII-XVIII a.): enciklopedinis žinynas (in Lithuanian). Vilnius: Mokslo ir enciklopedijų leidybos institutas. p. 31. ISBN 5-420-01535-8.
