3 TITL Meinhard II. – Wikipedia
3 NOTE Meinhard II
4 CONT
4 CONT
4 CONT Meinhard II, Count of Tyrol and Gorizia, Duke of Carinthia (historicizing engraving from the 18th century)
4 CONT Meinhard II (c. 1238 ; † November 1st 1295 in Greifenburg ) of the family of Meinhardiner was Count (as Meinhard IV) of Gorizia (1258-1267) and Tyrol , Duke of Carinthia (1286-1295), and pledge master of the Carniola and the Windi
4 CONC sch Mark . He was one of the most important rulers of the 13 Century and the founder of Tyrol as an independent country.
4 CONT As the son of Count Meinhard I dropped him to the County of Tyrol by inheritance 1271st His brother Albert I received the possessions in Friuli , Istria , Carinthia and in the Dolomites .
4 CONT He was famous for coining the "Zwainzigers," the first German large silver coin , which has been widely imitated. He supported Rudolph I of Habsburg in the dispute with King Ottokar II of Bohemia in 1286 and was for the princes ga
4 CONC thered and the Duchy of Carinthia invested.
4 CONT Together with his wife Elisabeth of Bavaria , widow of the Hohenstaufen Conrad IV , in 1284 he founded the Cistercian monastery of Stams in Tyrol, where he found his grave. His daughter Elizabeth married Albert I , the German king
4 CONC  of the years 1298 to 1308, and became the ancestress of all subsequent Habsburgs. Through its submission bishoprics of Trent and Brixen he is considered the real founder of the Tyrol.
4 CONT Descendants [ Edit ]
4 CONT
4 CONT Albert II, Count of Tyrol († 1292);
4 CONT Agnes († May 14, 1293) (married 1286 Frederick I (Meissen) )
4 CONT Elisabeth of Carinthia, Gorizia and Tyrol (1262-1313) (married King Albert I );
4 CONT Otto III. (around 1265-1310), Count of Gorizia and Tyrol, Duke of Carinthia and Carniola;
4 CONT Henry VI. (around 1270-1335), Duke of Carinthia, Duke / Margrave of Carniola, Count of Tyrol, King of Bohemia and titular King of Poland;
4 CONT Ludwig († 1305).
4 CONT Furthermore Meinhard had numerous extramarital offspring:
4 CONT Frederick, provost of Brixen († March 13, 1333), Anna Reichenberg married
4 CONT Henry, Earl of ash hole († 1349)
4 CONT Albrecht of Camian and Forestry, Viscount of Tyrol († 1335/36), with Floridiana (Siguna) of Schlandersberg married
4 CONT and another ten illegitimate children with hitherto unknown women
4 CONT Literature [ Edit ]
4 CONT
4 CONT Dream of a prince - the emergence of Tyrol. exhibition catalog of the exhibition at Castle Tyrol Tyrol and Stams, Dorf Tirol Innsbruck 1995.
4 CONT Wilhelm Baum: ., the Counts of Gorizia in European politics of the Middle Ages Kitab-Verlag, Klagenfurt, 2000. ISBN 3-902005-04-1
4 CONT Wilhelm Baum:  Meinhard II . In: New German Biography (NDB). Volume 16 Duncker & Humblot, Berlin 1990, ISBN 3-428-00197-4 , pp. 668 f ( digitized ).
4 CONT Alfons Huber:  Meinhard II . In: General German Biography (ADB). Volume 21, Duncker & Humblot, Leipzig, 1885, pp. 229-231.
4 CONT Hermann Wiesflecker: Meinhard the second. Tyrol, Carinthia and the neighboring countries of the 13th Century (Sciliar fonts 124). Wagner, Innsbruck 1995. ISBN 3-7030-0287-5
4 CONT External links 
