2 CONT
2 CONT Magnus I Herzog von Braunschweig1
2 CONT
2 CONT M, #4727, b. circa 1304, d. circa July 1369
2 CONT
2 CONT Last Edited=16 Jul 2005
2 CONT
2 CONT      Magnus I Herzog von Braunschweig was born circa 1304. He was the son of Albrecht II Herzog von Braunschweig-Göttingen and Rixa von Werle.1 He married Sophie von Brandenburg, daughter of Heinrich I Markgraf von Brandenburg and
2 CONC  Agnes von Bayern, circa 1327. He died circa July 1369.
2 CONT
2 CONT      Magnus I Herzog von Braunschweig was a member of the House of Guelph.1 Magnus I Herzog von Braunschweig also went by the nick-name of Magnus 'the Pious'.1 He succeeded to the title of Herzog von Braunschweig in 1345.1
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Magnus I Herzog von Braunschweig and Sophie von Brandenburg
2 CONT
2 CONT Sophie von Braunschweig+ d. c 1394
2 CONT
2 CONT Matilde von Braunschweig+ d. a 28 Jun 1354
2 CONT
2 CONT Ludwig Herzog von Braunschweig-Lüneburg d. c 1367
2 CONT
2 CONT Albrecht von Braunschweig d. 14 Apr 1395
2 CONT
2 CONT Heinrich von Braunschweig d. a 1382
2 CONT
2 CONT Ernst von Braunschweig d. a 16 Feb 1385
2 CONT
2 CONT Otto von Braunschweig d. 16 Jan 1339
2 CONT
2 CONT Magnus II Herzog von Braunschweig+1 b. c 1328, d. 26 Jul 1373
2 CONT
2 CONT Citations
2 CONT
2 CONT [S38] John Morby, Dynasties of the World: a chronological and genealogical handbook (Oxford, Oxfordshire, U.K.: Oxford University Press, 1989), page 132. Hereinafter cited as Dynasties of the World.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Magnus the Pious, Duke of Brunswick-Lüneburg
2 CONT
2 CONT The son of Albert the Fat, Duke of Brunswick-Lüneburg, Magnus was still a minor when his father died in 1318; he and his brother Ernest were put under the guardianship of their elder brother Otto, who continued as sole ruler even 
2 CONC after his brothers came of age. After marrying Sophie, a niece of Louis IV, Holy Roman Emperor, Magnus was appointed margrave of Landsberg and count palatine of Saxony by the Emperor in 1333. Magnus took residence at Sangerhausen.
2 CONC  When Otto died in 1344, Magnus and Ernest jointly took over government of the state; but already on 17 April 1345, they agreed to divide the territory. Magnus received the Principality of Wolfenbüttel.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1346, a border war between Wolfenbüttel and the Archbishop of Magdeburg broke out. In exchange for help in this conflict, Magnus sold the Margraviate of Landsberg to Frederick II, Margrave of Meißen. But the Archbishop conquere
2 CONC d Schöningen in 1347, and Magnus had to cede Hötensleben and some other possessions to the Archbishop. Financially ruined by the war, Magnus could not stop the cities in the state from acquiring more and more rights; especially th
2 CONC e City of Brunswick was becoming more powerful.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1348, the Emperor gave Landsberg and the Palatinate of Saxony to Bernard, Prince of Anhalt. The ensuing conflict over these territories between Magnus and Bernard ended amicably with arriage between Magnus' son Magnus and Cathe
2 CONC rine, daughter of Valdemar, Prince of Anhalt.
2 CONT
2 CONT Magnus attempted to secure the Principality of Lüneburg for his son Louis, so that it could be reunited with Wolfenbüttel. The prince of Lüneburg, William II, Duke of Brunswick-Lüneburg, a member of the same house to which Magnus 
2 CONC belonged, the House of Welf, did not have sons; however, he had already promised the principality to a son of his daughter, a relative of the Duke of Saxony, before he agreed to Magnus' plan. Louis then married William's daughter 
2 CONC Matilda. A lengthy conflict broke out that culminated in the Lüneburg Succession War, which was resolved only in 1388.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1367, Magnus joined Dietrich, Archbishop of Magdeburg, Albert, Bishop of Halberstadt, Valdemar, Prince of Anhalt, and others in a campaign against Gerhard of Berg, Bishop of Hildesheim; they were defeated by Hildesheim in a bat
2 CONC tle near Farmsen and Dinklar on 3 September. Magnus was taken prisoner, and had to buy his freedom. He died in Summer of 1369.
2 CONT
2 CONT Around 1327, Magnus married Sophie (died 1356), daughter of Henry I, Margrave of Brandenburg. They had the following children that reached adulthood:
2 CONT
2 CONT Magnus (died 1373) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Louis (died 1367) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Albert, Archbishop of Bremen (died 1395) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry, Provost of Halberstadt Cathedral 
2 CONT
2 CONT Ernest 
2 CONT
2 CONT Matilda, married Bernard III, Prince of Anhalt 
2 CONT
2 CONT Helen, married Otto, Count of Hoya 
2 CONT
2 CONT Agnes, married Eric, Count of Hoya 
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT "Kegyes"Magnus Braunschweig
