1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eberhard_I._(Katzenelnbogen)
2 CONT
2 CONT Count Eberhard I of Katzenelnbogen (b. c. 1243;[1] † 23 August 1311) was co-ruler of the County of Katzenelnbogen and founder of the younger line of Katzenelnbogen. After the division in 1260, his possessions were increasingly in 
2 CONC the Upper County of Katzenelnbogen, while his brother Diether V (founder of the Elder Line) had more property in the Lower County of Katzenelnbogen.
2 CONT
2 CONT Family
2 CONT Eberhard I was the son of Count Diether IV of Katzenelnbogen and his wife Hildegunde of Eberstein. He ruled as the first lord of the younger line from 1260 to 1311 and was married to Elisabeth von Eppstein. They had the following 
2 CONC children:[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Diether († 1289)
2 CONT Philip († 1290)
2 CONT Gerhard (b. 1293; † 1312), ⚭ 25 January 1299[2] Margaret of the Mark, daughter of Eberhard I of the Mark
2 CONT John II
2 CONT Bertha (b. 1283; † after 1307), ⚭ Thomas of Rieneck
2 CONT Margaret (b. 1295)
2 CONT Berthold III († 1321), ⚭ Adelheid von Sayn
2 CONT Life
2 CONT In 1260, the county of Katzenelnbogen was divided between Eberhard I and his brother Diether V. From then on, Diether V presided over the Lower County of Katzenelnbogen and St. Goar with Rheinfels Castle, which became the residenc
2 CONC e of the older line, while the Upper County around Darmstadt was awarded to Count Eberhard I and thus to the younger line. Diether V also had property in the Upper County. In 1274, Zwingenberg, which belonged to him, was granted t
2 CONC own rights by King Rudolf of Habsburg. This made Zwingenberg the oldest town on the Bergstraße.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1273 Eberhard I entered the service of King Rudolf, became his advisory friend, had his full confidence and was very often a member of his entourage. He also remained loyal to his successor Adolf of Nassau, who only came to the
2 CONC  court of King Rudolf through the mediation of Eberhard. As an advisor, he was an important pillar of imperial policy on the Rhine for the two German kings. With his help, the interregnum was quickly ended, and the destruction of 
2 CONC over seventy robber baron castles was partly due to him. At the Battle of Göllheim on 2 July 1298 against the army of Albrecht of Austria, Adolf of Nassau was killed and Eberhard was captured. He traveled around the empire for his
2 CONC  kings, and the documents mentioning his name are numerous. [3]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1276, Eberhard I secured the imperial pledge to Trebur. In 1282 he was pledged to the Boppard customs, in 1283 he bought the castle, town and customs of Braubach, and in 1291 he had Stadeck Castle built.
2 CONT
2 CONT After his death in 1311, Eberhard I was buried in the church of Eberbach Abbey. He was succeeded as Count of Katzenelenbogen by his son Gerhard.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://kloster-eberbach.de/en/vor-ort/klosterrundgang/grabplatten/graf-eberhard-i-von-katzenelnbogen
2 CONT https://www.deutsche-biographie.de/sfz42006.html#ndbcontent
2 CONT https://gw.geneanet.org/fraloy?lang=en&n=von+katzenelnbogen&p=eberhard+i.
2 CONT https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stammliste_des_Hauses_Katzenelnbogen
2 CONT https://familypedia.fandom.com/wiki/Eberhard_I._von_Katzenelnbogen_(c1231-1311)
