1 NAME Albrecht VII /von Mansfeld-Hinterort/ VII
2 GIVN Albrecht VII
2 SURN von Mansfeld-Hinterort
2 NSFX VII
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 18 JUN 1480
2 PLAC Kranichfeld,Sachsen-Meiningn,Thuringia, Germany
2 ADDR
1 DEAT
2 DATE 4 MAR 1560
2 ADDR
3 CITY Leutenberg
3 STAE TH
3 POST 07338
3 CTRY Germany
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://www.genealogics.org/getperson.php?personID=I00036363&tree=LEO
2 CONT
2 CONT and extracted from there:
2 CONT
2 CONT "Albrecht was a close friend of Martin Luther, who came from the county of Mansfeld, and he became a fervent follower of the Reformation movement. By 1518 he had come to know Luther personally. A year later Albrecht and his brothe
2 CONC r Gebhard, who lived at Schloss Seeburg, openly joined the Reformation movement. Albrecht was among the first of the German nobles who chose Protestantism despite great risk to themselves, and it would cost him dearly. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1521 Luther dedicated a book to Albrecht in which he wrote: 'May God's mercy protect the count for the teaching of men and the worship of God'."
2 CONT
2 CONT and later:
2 CONT
2 CONT "Shortly after Luther's death in 1546 - Albrecht was present at his death in his residence in Eisleben - the Schmalkaldic war broke out. Albrecht and his sons fought on the side of the Protestant princes, while other counts of Man
2 CONC sfeld, among them Albrecht's Protestant brother Gebhard, fought on the side of the Catholic emperor. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In October 1542 Albrecht lost his son Caspar in battle. At Christmas 1546 he returned to the county of Mansfeld, where he quickly conquered the estates of his Catholic relatives. In 1547 Albrecht was appointed by Moritz, Kurfürst 
2 CONC von Sachsen, to be commander in chief of the army defending Bremen against the Catholic forces. In May 1547 Albrecht and his direct descendants were stripped by the emperor of all their wealth and properties. These were given to A
2 CONC lbrecht's Catholic relatives and particularly his fellow-Protestant brother Gebhard."
