1 NAME Iso /von Wölpe/
2 GIVN Iso
2 _MARNM von Wölpe
1 NAME Yso/Ÿso
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE ABT 1147
1 DEAT
2 DATE 5 AUG 1231
1 OCCU Bischof von Verden/Prince-Bishop of Verden (an der Aller) from 1205-1231, Reichsfürst (Imperial Prince with "imperial immediacy"), and Adliger (nobleman).
1 TITL Bischof
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''ENTRY FROM FEMINAE:'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Bishop Iso von Wölpe (ca. 1170–1231) rose to a position of considerable power in the early 13th-century Holy Roman Empire. Beginning in 1205, as prince-bishop of Verden, a town in what is now the northwest German state of Lower Sa
2 CONC xony, he wielded great spiritual and secular authority during a time of immense political and religious tumult. In his role as prince-bishop, Iso enjoyed imperial immediacy, meaning he was an elector of the Holy Roman Emperor and,
2 CONC  by default, operated largely independently of the emperor (and to an extent, the pope). This synthesis of ecclesiastical and lay authority in one local ruler was a defining feature of 13th-century imperial politics in the Germani
2 CONC es. Iso's monumental brass, dedicated upon his death in 1231, is the oldest remaining medieval brass monument in Europe.
2 CONT
2 CONT The inscription around the edge of the brass reads: "Iso von Wölpe, thirty-first Bishop of Verden ruled for 26 years. He built the monastery of Saint Andrew. He was the first to fortify Verden. He dissolved the lordship of the cit
2 CONC y and expedited the relinquishing of control of the monks’ goods held by "dynasten" like the von Wanebergen/Waneberge/Wahnebergen (Advocati/Vogtei zu Verden) and the extinct von Westen "dynasten". Having bought the patrimony of We
2 CONC sten for 500 marks and more, he donated it to Saint Mary. He died happily in the year of the Lord’s incarnation MCCXXXI on the nones of August."
2 CONT
2 CONT His effigy shows him dressed in full liturgical vestments: he wears an alb, dalmatic, chasuble, pallium, and on his head, a mitre. The subdued patterns decorating his priestly garments bring the viewer’s eyes up to meet Iso’s stro
2 CONC ng gaze. A crosier, a symbol of the bishop’s role as shepherd of his flock, leans against his right side. In each hand he holds a model of a building: in his right, St. Andrew's Church, which he founded in 1220, and in his left, t
2 CONC he city wall of Verden, which he commissioned during his episcopate, and which demarcated what would later become the town’s boundaries. This representation aptly illustrates his power as both a religious and lay ruler over the to
2 CONC wn.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Related Resources:'''
2 CONT "Iso von Wolpe" in ''Germania Sacra''. http://personendatenbank.germania-sacra.de/index/gnd/136953913. Accessed 2016;
2 CONT
2 CONT Norris, M. W. “The Schools of Brasses in Germany.” ''Journal of the British Archaeological Association'' 3rd series 19 (1956): 34-52;
2 CONT
2 CONT Vogtherr, Thomas. ''Iso von Wölpe, Bischof von Verden: (1205-1231); Reichsfürst, Bischof, Adliger; eine Biographie''. Landschaftsverband der Ehemaligen Herzogtümer Bremen und Verden, 2008.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://inpress.lib.uiowa.edu/feminae/DetailsPage.aspx?Feminae_ID=32552
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT '''THE COUNTY OF WÖLPE:'''
2 CONT
2 CONT The County of Wölpe (German: Grafschaft Wölpe) was the territorial lordship of a noble family in the Middle Ages in the Middle Weser Region near Nienburg/Weser which folded in 1302. The seat of the counts of Wölpe was the castle s
2 CONC ite at Erichshagen-Wölpe on the Wölpe stream in the borough of Nienburg in north Germany. The castle itself no longer exists.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Important Representatives'''
2 CONT 1. Bernard II of Wölpe (1176–1221), founder of Neustadt am Rübenberge and Mariensee Abbey
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Iso of Wölpe (1167–1231), Prince-Bishop of Verden
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Sources'''
2 CONT Marcus René Duensing: Die Chronik der County of Wölpe, Diepenau 1999, ISBN 3-929793-69-5
2 CONT
2 CONT Ernst Andreas Friedrich: Wenn Steine reden könnten. Band IV, Landbuch-Verlag, Hannover 1998, ISBN 3-7842-0558-5
2 CONT
2 CONT Dieter Riemer: counts und Herren im Erzstift Bremen im Spiegel der Geschichte Lehes, Bremerhaven/Hamburg 1995 (Diss. phil. Oldenburg) S. 141 ff ISBN 3-923725-89-2
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.erichshagen-woelpe.de/geschichte/grafen.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT https://kulturerbe.niedersachsen.de/piresolver?id=isil_DE-MUS-163517_kenom_3433
2 CONT
2 CONT ____________________________________________________________________
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Er war der jüngere Sohn des Grafen Bernhard I. von Wölpe aus der Grafschaft Wölpe. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Iso war 1188 Domherr in Verden und Propst von Bardowick. Ab 1197 war er Dompropst. 1205 wurde er zum Bischof von Verden gewählt. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Das Bild Iso von Wölpes auf seiner Grabplatte in der Verdener St. Andreas-Kirche.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iso_von_W%C3%B6lpe
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://www.geni.com/people/Gerhard-de-Hoya-Bischof/6000000022435424586 Gerhard de Hoya], Bischof zu Verden 1254 is Iso von Wölpe's great nephew.
