1 NOTE {geni:about_me} == Jutta von Blankenburg==
2 CONT
2 CONT * Daughter of Siegfried III, Graf von Blankenburg and Mathilde von Wöltingerode-Woldenberg 
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://de.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Otto_von_Hadmersleben Otto von Hadmersleben, by Wikipedia]
2 CONT
2 CONT The name of the nobles of Hadmersleben is derived from the headquarters in the Hadmersleben castle on the edge of the river . In 1250 Otto von Hadmersleben conquered the town and castle of Egeln and founded a new line of nobles fr
2 CONC om Hadmersleben zu Egeln there. According to Leuckfeld , Otto von Hadmersleben was always ready for a feud, and the monk Reinold von Marienthal called him the "devil on earth". In 1259 he and his wife Jutta von Blankenburg founded
2 CONC  the Marienstuhl monastery in Egeln. The monastery donors are buried in front of the main altar of the monastery church.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://de.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kloster_Marienstuhl Marienstuhl Monastery]
2 CONT
2 CONT The Marienstuhl Monastery (Sedis S. Marie) before Egeln was a Cistercian convent in what is now the German state of Saxony-Anhalt .
2 CONT
2 CONT At the instigation of his wife, Jutta von Blankenburg, the noble lord Otto von Hadmersleben founded the Marienstuhl Monastery on March 14, 1259 , so that consecrated virgins should serve there according to the rule of St. Bernard 
2 CONC of Clairvaux in the Order of Cisterz. The first abbess was Mechthild von Blankenburg, the sister of Countess Jutta. The buildings of the former Cistercian convent Marienstuhl, which are still preserved today, were erected in the y
2 CONC ears 1696–1734; [1]
2 CONT
2 CONT The monastery soon gained economic independence through extensive foundations. Since the rule of the Cistercians, in addition to devotion and prayer, also prescribes gardening, the monastery cultivated and raised cattle. There was
2 CONC  also a medicinal herb farm , a pharmacy , an infirmary , a parament embroidery , the monastery school as well as fishing , brewery , dairy and bakery .
2 CONT
2 CONT With the introduction of the Reformation in 1547, the town of Egeln became Protestant, the provosts and some of the nuns tended to Lutheranism , but the monastery stuck to the old faith. In the years 1577 to 1730 the monastery chu
2 CONC rch was shared by the Protestant Altemarkt community. [2]
2 CONT
2 CONT From 1630 to 1633 Anna Margareta von Haugwitz lived here as a pupil of the convent , who had become an orphan at the age of eight after a massacre by the imperial troops in Calbe (Saale) . When the Swedish general Johann Banér res
2 CONC ided in the renaissance castle Egeln for some time from 1632 , a lady from his entourage found out the widowed Countess Elisabeth Juliane von Löwenstein-Scharfeneck , née Countess von Erbach(Erpach), from fate, accepted the girl f
2 CONC rom the convent and as her own foster daughter. At the age of 18, Anna Margareta married a Unterführer Banérs, the 27-year-old Swedish general , later Reichsmarschall and Count von Salmis , Karl Gustav Wrangel .
2 CONT
2 CONT Under the abbess Maria Zeiseler and the provost Christoph Jordan, the entire monastery complex, including farm buildings and monastery wall, was rebuilt between 1696 and 1719. In 1730, under the abbess Katharina Musäus, a new chur
2 CONC ch was built for the Protestant community of Altemarkt (now Katharinenkapelle at the cemetery) and then the construction of the monastery church began. For this purpose, the old Gothic monastery church was torn down to its foundat
2 CONC ion walls and rebuilt, enlarged by a third, so that it is out of the cloister square towards the eaststood out. The construction began in 1732 under the Abbess Katharina Musäus and was completed in 1734 under the Abbess Anna Marga
2 CONC retha Müller. Up until 1738, the artists had to complete the unique baroque interior that is still preserved today. On the nun gallery there is a Gothic statue of the Virgin Mary (around 1450), which is important as the Madonna of
2 CONC  Mercy and pilgrimage for pilgrims. In the lower church there is a Gothic altar cross (around 1330), a late Romanesque sandstone Madonna (around 1260), a Pietà (around 1450) and a Renaissance baptismal font (1605). The furnishings
2 CONC  include chalices , monstrances (including 1728) and vestments from the 18th – 20th centuries. Century.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1769, the monastery received for its willingness to build 30 houses for Huguenots expelled from France , from King Friedrich II. Final permission for the free election of provosts and Catholic pastoral work for the city of Egel
2 CONC n and the surrounding area.
2 CONT
2 CONT By decree of the King of Westphalia , Jerome Bonaparte , of 13 May 1809, the resolution following monasteries were ordered Klostermarienberg chair in front of leeches, [2] Kloster Wöltingerode in Vienenburg , St. Burchardi in Halb
2 CONC erstadt , vein life in ways of life , Teistungenburg in Eichsfeld and Hadmersleben and sold to fill his war chest and at the same time stipulated that the conventuals and lay sisters should be transferred to the other, still exist
2 CONC ing nunneries.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://foerderverein-wasserburg-egeln.jimdofree.com/historie/ '''The Wasserburg zu Egeln''']
2 CONT
2 CONT The Niederungsburg, surrounded by water, on the eastern outskirts of Egeln was first mentioned in 1207 as the property of the Gernrode Monastery and was conquered by the nobles of Hadmersleben in 1250. The best-known representativ
2 CONC e of the family was Otto von Hadmersleben who was married to Countess Jutta von Blankenburg and, according to the chronicler Leuckfeld, was always ready for a feud. He had the place surrounded by a strong wall with 2 city gates an
2 CONC d strengthened the fortifications of the castle. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1259 Jutta and Otto von Hadmersleben founded the Marienstuhl Monastery in “their town of Egeln”.
2 CONT The lords of Hadmersleben ruled the Egeln rulership for 166 years until they died out in the male line with Count Kurt von Egeln in 1416. Since the Counts of Hadmersleben zu Egeln concluded a hereditary brotherhood with the Counts
2 CONC  of Barby-Mühlingen in 1375, Egeln now came into the hands of the Magdeburg Cathedral Chapter via the Counts of Barby-Mühlingen and was used as a summer residence and table goods. From 1430 an extensive phase of renovation and exp
2 CONC ansion of the castle complex began.
2 CONT
2 CONT The villages, lands and the city of Egeln belonging to the castle were administered by the cathedral capital office and by a bailiff who also exercised higher jurisdiction. During the Thirty Years' War, the castle was besieged and
2 CONC  captured several times by Swedes and imperialists. In 1631 the Swedish King Gustav Adolf gave the offices of Hadmersleben and Egeln to Field Marshal Johann Banèr for his services in the Battle of Breitenfels, and in 1632 Banér an
2 CONC d his family moved into Egeln Castle. After the Peace of Westphalia, the castle became a Prussian domain and leased to deserving officers who also exercised the function of royal bailiff. Above all, the Wilhelm and Friederike Wahn
2 CONC schaffe family should be mentioned here, who held the office of leeches in the difficult times of the Napoleonic wars. In addition to the changing crews, where up to 200 soldiers had to be quartered and fed at times, famous people
2 CONC  such as Queen Luise of Prussia, Count Bernadotte, Count Murat and General Blücher and their entourage were also visiting. After 1945 the complex was a state-owned property, but there was little interest in preserving the historic
2 CONC  buildings, so a period of increasing decline began.
2 CONT
