1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Henryk III głogowski''', także Henryk III Głogowczyk – '''książę głogowski''' (1273/1274-1309), '''książę wielkopolski''' (1306-1309). [http://pl.wikipedia.org/wiki/Henryk_III_g%C5%82ogowski Wikipedia PL]
2 CONT
2 CONT Wikipedia:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Henry_III,_Duke_of_Silesia-Glogau
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry III, Duke of Silesia-Glogau
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry III (I) of Glogów (Polish: Henryk III głogowski; b. 1251/60 - d. 3 December 1309) was a Duke of Glogów (Glogau) from 1274 to his death and also Duke of parts of Greater Poland during 1306-1309.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the eldest son of Konrad I, Duke of Glogów, by his first wife Salome, daughter of Duke Władysław of Greater Poland.
2 CONT
2 CONT Contents
2 CONT
2 CONT [show]
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 1 Life
2 CONT
2 CONT           o 1.1 Early years
2 CONT
2 CONT           o 1.2 Beginning of cooperation with Henry IV Probus
2 CONT
2 CONT           o 1.3 Fall of King Ottokar II and Division of the Duchy of Głogów
2 CONT
2 CONT           o 1.4 Influence of Henry IV Probus over Silesia
2 CONT
2 CONT           o 1.5 Henry IV's death and execution of his will
2 CONT
2 CONT           o 1.6 War against Henry V the Fat
2 CONT
2 CONT           o 1.7 Death of Przemysł II and the succession over Greater Poland
2 CONT
2 CONT           o 1.8 War against Bolko I the Strict
2 CONT
2 CONT           o 1.9 Relations with King Wenceslaus II of Bohemia
2 CONT
2 CONT           o 1.10 Disputes with Bishop Jan Romka and Konrad II the Hunchback
2 CONT
2 CONT           o 1.11 Increase of Henry III's power. Adoption of the title "Heir of the Kingdom of Poland"
2 CONT
2 CONT           o 1.12 Claims over Greater Poland. Acquisition of parts from Przemysł II's inheritance
2 CONT
2 CONT           o 1.13 Internal politics
2 CONT
2 CONT           o 1.14 Death and succession
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 2 Marriage and issue
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 3 External links
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Life
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Early years
2 CONT
2 CONT Little is known about his first years of life. In 1267 Henry III participated in the canonization of his great-grandmother Hedwig of Andechs. At the time of his father's death in 1274 he was still a minor.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Beginning of cooperation with Henry IV Probus
2 CONT
2 CONT The first participation of Henry III in the political arena was in 1277, when together with Przemysł II of Greater Poland he took part in an armed expedition against his uncle Bolesław II the Bald. The trip was provoked by the att
2 CONC itude of the Duke of Legnica, who, in order to obtain territorial concessions, kidnapped the young Henry IV and imprisoned him in his castle of Legnica. Henry III and the Bohemian King Ottokar II formed a coalition of Silesian Pri
2 CONC nces to help the prisoner Duke. However, they were defeated in the Battle of Stolec on 24 April 1277. At the head of the Legnica troops came the son and heir of Bolesław II, Henry V the Fat. It was probably here that the conflicts
2 CONC  between him and Henry V began, which later caused great suffering to the Duke of Legnica.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Fall of King Ottokar II and Division of the Duchy of Głogów
2 CONT
2 CONT One year later Henry III participated in the great Battle on the Marchfeld (26 August 1278), where King Ottokar II was defeated and killed. In the same year Henry III was forced to made a division of his lands between his brothers
2 CONC : he retained the main cities of the Duchy -Głogów, Bytom Odrzański and Kożuchów-; Konrad II the Hunchback obtained Ścinawa and Przemko received Żagań and Nowogród Bobrzański.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Influence of Henry IV Probus over Silesia
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1281 Henry III, together with Henry V the Fat and Przemysł II of Greater Poland were invited by Henry IV Probus to a meeting in Sądowel. The Duke of Wrocław broke the law of hospitality and captured the three Dukes. Henry IV re
2 CONC leased them only after they recognized his overlordship. Although the agreement was concluded under duress, it has stood the test of time and in the next few years, we see Henry III, together with his brother Przemko among the clo
2 CONC se associates of Prince Wroclaw. Henryk III Probusa supported primarily Thomas II, bishop of Wroclaw in the great political ecclesiastical conflict. It was only after the death of Henry III that Henry IV's relations with the clerg
2 CONC y returned to normal.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Henry IV's death and execution of his will
2 CONT
2 CONT The close cooperation between Henry III and Henryk IV Probus was evident when in Krosno Odrzańskie on 11 January 1288 Henry III was knighted by the Duke of Wrocław. The major proof of the subjugation of the Duke of Glogów was afte
2 CONC r the death of his brother Przemko on 26 February 1289: his Duchy of Ścinawa was anexed by Henry IV without any protest of Henry III. However, one year later, on 23 June 1290, Henry IV Probus died suddenly, probably poisoned, an e
2 CONC vent which seriously affected the further career of the Duke of Glogów. In his will, the dying Duke of Wrocław left Henry III as his main heir. However, the Wrocław knights and burghers had other plans and one month later (mid-Jul
2 CONC y 1290) they forced Henry III to escape. As their new ruler, the rebels invited Duke Henry V the Fat of Legnica to take the government. The reasons for the Wrocław revolt were unknown, but maybe the harsh rule of Henry III was a d
2 CONC ecisive factor. In any event, Henry III refused to accept this and immediately declared war on Henry V.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] War against Henry V the Fat
2 CONT
2 CONT Shortly after taking control over Wrocław, Henry V the Fat forced the Duke of Głogów to renounce all his pretentions over the disputed territories of Chojnów, Boleslawiec, Gościszów, Nowogrodziec, Ścinawa, Wińsko, Syców, Uraz, Trz
2 CONC ebnica, Milicz and Sądowel. However, Henry III managed to recover Ścinawa soon after.
2 CONT
2 CONT Both sides quickly began to find allies who would support them in the war. Henry III made a treaty with the House of Wettin, strengthening this alliance by marrying the daughter of Duke Albert I of Brunswick-Lüneburg, Matilda (Mar
2 CONC ch 1291). Further allies were Otto IV, Margrave of Brandenburg-Stendal and Przemysł II of Greater Poland. With the latter Henry III entered into an arrangement under which if Przemysł II died without male issue, the Duke of Głogów
2 CONC  would inherit his lands. In the meanwhile, Henry V the Fat obtained the support of King Wenceslaus II of Bohemia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry V's rule over Wrocław was extremely conservative and clashed with some political opponents among the nobility. One of them, Pakosław Zdzieszyca, was sentenced to death accused of murder. The revenge of Pakosław's son Lutka, 
2 CONC was impressive: on 11 November 1293 he captured Henry V and gave him to Henry III, who put him in an iron cage for almost six months. Henry V only obtained his freedom after surrendering the towns of Namysłów, Bierutów, Oleśnica, 
2 CONC Kluczbork, Byczyna, Wołczyn, Olesno, Chojnów and Bolesławiec (almost 1/3 of Henry IV's lands) with their respectives fortresses to Henry III, paying of a ransom of 30,000 pieces of silver and promising to assist the Duke of Głogów
2 CONC  for the next five years in all his wars.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Death of Przemysł II and the succession over Greater Poland
2 CONT
2 CONT On 8 February 1296 the Duke of Greater Poland and since 1295 King of Poland Przemysł II was murdered. The Greater Poland-Głogów alliance collapsed around 1293, when Przemysł II attacked Duke Władysław I the Elbow-high of Kuyavia. 
2 CONC Because of this, the Greater Poland nobility chose Władysław I to succeeded Przemysł II in Poznań, despite the claims of Henry III, who considered that the treaty signed with Przemysł II in 1290 was still valid and in consequence,
2 CONC  he was his sole heir (Przemysł II married three times but only left one daughter from his second marriage, Richeza).
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry III and Władysław I quickly concluded a settlement on 10 March 1296 in Krzywin, under which the Duke of Głogów obtained all the lands from the south of the Obra River. At the same time Władysław I designated Henry III's elde
2 CONC st son, Henry IV the Faithful as his heir over Poznań, and, in case of his death without male issue, over all Greater Poland.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] War against Bolko I the Strict
2 CONT
2 CONT The reason why Henry III gave large concessions to Władysław I in the treaty of 1296 was because he was already defeated by Bolko I the Strict, Duke of Jawor. When Henry III stayed in Krzywin, Bolko I took Chojnów and Boleslawiec.
2 CONC  Through the mediation of King Wenceslaus II of Bohemia, a settlement was made on Zwanowice in March 1297; the Duke of Głogów managed to made a truce with the Duke of Jawor, but was forced to renounce the above-mentioned districts
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Relations with King Wenceslaus II of Bohemia
2 CONT
2 CONT At the end of the 1290s Henry III could improve his relations with the King Wenceslaus II of Bohemia (for example, he was present in Prague on Wenceslaus II's coronation in 1297), but also his relations with Władysław I the Elbow-
2 CONC high deteriorated, and at the end a war broke out. In June 1298, in Kościan (and despite the opposition of the Greater Poland nobility with the Bishop of Poznan Andrzej Zaremba at their head) Henry III and Władysław I signed a new
2 CONC  treaty, under which in return for confirmation of the church and the inclusion in a future "Kingdom" of the functions of Chancellor, Henry III promised assistance to Władysław I in order to complete the total subjection of Greate
2 CONC r Poland, Gdańsk Pomerania and in this way he could obtain the Royal crown. The conflict between the Piast Dukes was used by King Wenceslaus II. In the village of Klęka on 23 August 1299 an agreement was concluded under which Henr
2 CONC y III was forced to abandon his pretentions over all Greater Poland for good.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Disputes with Bishop Jan Romka and Konrad II the Hunchback
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry III accepted immediately the intervention of the Bohemian King, because he was busy in his own duchy with his disputes with the Bishop of Wroclaw Jan Romka. The conflict was caused by the Duchy of Żagań, which, previously be
2 CONC longing to his younger brother Konrad II the Hunchback, was taken by Henry III in March 1299 when Konrad II was chosen as Patriarch of Aquileia. But when Konrad II returned to Żagań, Henryk III didn't want to return the Duchy to h
2 CONC im, which was under his rule during his absence. Even after the intervention of the vassals and the Church, Henry III refused to give Konrad II his Duchy. Bishop Jan Romka excommunicated him and supported the requests of Konrad II
2 CONC . The fight with the church was resolved only on 24 April 1300 confirming the victory of the Bishop and the restitution of Żagań to Konrad II.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Increase of Henry III's power. Adoption of the title "Heir of the Kingdom of Poland"
2 CONT
2 CONT The Duke of Glogów didn't have any intention of resigning his pretentions over the Greater Poland inheritance, as was evidenced in 1301, when he adopted the title: "Heir of the Polish Kingdom, Duke of Silesia, Głogów and Poznań" (
2 CONC Polish: "dziedzic Królestwa Polskiego, książę Śląska, pan Głogowa i Poznania"). This soon caused an armed conflict with the King of Bohemia and now of Poland Wenceslaus II. The danger around Henry III increased especially after 13
2 CONC 01, when the Bohemian King took over the custody of the children of Henry V the Fat, this after the death on 9 November 1301 of their uncle Bolko I the Strict. Despite the delicate situation the war never occurred, because Wencesl
2 CONC aus II was busy in his attempts to obtain the Hungarian crown for his son. The death of Wenceslaus II in 1305 and the murdere of his son and successor Wenceslaus III the following year favored the opportunities of Henry III over h
2 CONC is pretentions in Greater Poland. The death of his brother Konrad II on 11 October 1304 further facilitated Henry III's situation, because he could annex his Duchy of Żagań without inconvenience and also reunified the whole Duchy 
2 CONC of Głogów under his rule.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Claims over Greater Poland. Acquisition of parts from Przemysł II's inheritance
2 CONT
2 CONT In the spring of 1306 Henry III was able to advance over the Greater Poland-Kuyavia borderline (Konin) and Gdańsk Pomerania, forcing Władysław I the Elbow-high to retreat. Kalisz resisted the pretentions of the Duke of Głogów, but
2 CONC  he was able to master it in 1307 from his current ruler, Duke Bolesław III the Generous (the eldest son of Henry V the Fat), who also had a claim over the whole succession of Wenceslaus (as the husband of her youngest daughter Ma
2 CONC rgareta); eventually, all Greater Poland was taken by the Duke of Głogów. Soon Henry III and the new King of Bohemia Henry of Carinthia formed an alliance against Bolesław III's ambitious plans.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Internal politics
2 CONT
2 CONT In the internal politics, Henry III could maintain his Duchy far away from the wars where he was constantly involved, and in consequence, the economy improved and with this the prestige and wealth of Głogów increased. Henry III al
2 CONC so introduced administrative and monetary reforms which affected not only the townspeople but also his own court and the nobility. Also, he founded more than a dozen urban centers (like Góra, Wąsosz, Polkowice, Twardogóra, Sulechó
2 CONC w, Zielona Góra, Lubin, Przemęt and Kościan).
2 CONT
2 CONT In his relations with the Church, Henry III was a generous benefactor. During his rule several monasteries and parish churches were founded. The Duke of Głogów also patron of many artists, which is reflected in the building of the
2 CONC  tombstones of Henry IV Probus, Bolesław I the Tall, his brothers and parents, in the Głogów Kolegiata and Przemysł II in Poznan Cathedral.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Death and succession
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry III died on 9 December 1309 and was buried in the Cistercian Church of Lubiąż. His five sons: Henry IV, Konrad I, Jan, Boleslaw and Przemko, soon split between them the inheritance of his father and led to the decline of the
2 CONC  Duchy. Moreover, this political fragmentation, visible especially in the Greater Poland region, scared the local nobility, who thought that maybe they risked losing their positions. In the end, Władysław I the Elbow-high was able
2 CONC  to conquer all Greater Poland in 1314.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Marriage and issue
2 CONT
2 CONT In March 1291, Henry III married Matilda (b. 1276 - d. 26 April 1318), daughter of Albert I, Duke of Brunswick-Lüneburg. They had nine children:
2 CONT
2 CONT    1. Henry IV the Faithful (b. ca. 1292 - d. 22 January 1342).
2 CONT
2 CONT    2. Konrad I (b. ca. 1294 - d. 22 December 1366).
2 CONT
2 CONT    3. Bolesław (b. ca. 1295 - d. bef. 23 April 1321).
2 CONT
2 CONT    4. Agnes (b. ca. 1296 - d. 25 December 1361), married firstly on 18 May 1309 to Otto III, Duke of Bavaria and secondly in 1329 to Alram, Count of Hals.
2 CONT
2 CONT    5. Salome (b. ca. 1297 - d. bef. 9 December 1309).
2 CONT
2 CONT    6. Jan (b. ca. 1298 - d. by 19 May 1365).
2 CONT
2 CONT    7. Katharina (b. ca. 1300 - d. 5 December 1323/26), married firstly bef. 24 March 1317 to John V, Margrave of Brandenburg-Salzwedel and secondly by 27 January 1319 to John III, Duke of Holstein.
2 CONT
2 CONT    8. Przemko II (b. ca. 1305 - d. 11 January 1331).
2 CONT
2 CONT    9. Hedwig (b. ca. 1308 - d. bef. December 1309).
2 CONT
2 CONT In his will, Henry III left his wife Glogów as her Oprawa wdowia, which she ruled until her own death.
2 CONT
