1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Hildegard was a Ludowinger who were the ruling dynasty of Thuringia and Hesse during the 11th to 13th centuries.  Their progenitor was Louis the Bearded who was descended from a noble family whose genealogy cannot 
2 CONC be precisely determined.  They had a close relationship with the Archbishopric of Mainz and also had estates on the Middle Main.  The male line of Ludovingians was extinguished on the death of Henry Raspe in 1247, leading to the W
2 CONC ar of the Thuringian Succession.  The castle of Schauenburg played a pivotal role.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hildegarde also was styled Gräfin Zu Nordeck Von Schauenburg.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Ludowingers adopted the Coat of Arms which were of the House of Hesse, Landgraves of Hesse.  
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000221675664916&size=medium}}
2 CONT The Ludovingian coat of arms showed a lion rampant barry (variously of seven, eight, nine or ten; shown here: of eight) argent and gules, the so-called lion of Hesse.
2 CONT …..
2 CONT From Medlands, Hildegarde is shown as daughter of LUDWIG "Cum-barba", Graf in Thuringia and CÄCILIE von Sangerhausen…
2 CONT 3.         HILDEGARD (-bur Goseck).   The Historia Brevis Principum Thuringiæ names (in order) "Hildegardis…Uta…Adelheidis" as the three daughters of "Ludewicus Cum-barba" & his wife, specifying that Hildegard married firstly "Bop
2 CONC poni comiti de Hennenberc" and secondly "Timoni de Nordeke", naming "Gebehardum qui Cellam Sancti Blasii ad monasterium Reinhersburn" as her son by her second husband[145].  The Chronicon Gozecense names "Hildegardis comitissa cui
2 CONC us frater comes fuit Ludewicus", recording her burial at Goseck monastery, and also specifying that "filium suum Domino vovit, Gebehardum scilicet" but that he left the monastery and was killed[146].  m firstly POPPO [I] Graf von 
2 CONC Henneberg, son of --- (-killed in battle near Melrichstadt 7 Aug 1078).  m secondly THIMO von Nordeck, son of ---.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hildegarde is buried at Goseck, a monastery, originally the Monastery St. Mary and St. Michael, built on the foundations of a castle, as well as the vineyard of Dechantenberg is located in the municipality of Goseck of Saxony-Anha
2 CONC lt in Germany.  Goseck Castle was part of a network of Frankish castles on the Saale River. It was founded around the year 800.
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000221676021900&size=large}}
2 CONT ''Goseck Castle above the Saale river, photo by Uwe Rieschel''
2 CONT …..
2 CONT Europasche Stamtafeln neue folge vol.1 tafel 129;
2 CONT …..
2 CONT (AI Summary My Heritage Family Trees)
2 CONT Hildegarde van Henneberg (born van Schauenburg), 1059 - 1104
2 CONT '''Birth'''
2 CONT Hildegarde van Henneberg (born van Schauenburg) was born in 1059, in Thüringen, Deutschland(HRR), to Ludwig "der Bärtige" von Thüringen and Cäcilie von Thüringen (born von Sangerhausen).
2 CONT '''Siblings'''
2 CONT Hildegarde had 8 siblings: Ludwig Christoph von Thüringen, Uta von Schauenburg und Thüringen, Beringer von Sangerhausen, Adelheid von Wippra (born von Thüringen), Dietrich von Lora, Uda von Linderbach (born von Thüringen), Adelhei
2 CONC d Von Wippra (born Von Thüringen) and Unknown Von Schauenburg Und Thüringen.
2 CONT '''Spouse'''
2 CONT Hildegarde married Poppo van Henneberg. Poppo was born in 1040, in Babenburg, Bamberg, Stift Bamberg, Deutschland(HRR). They had 2 sons: Gotebold von Henneberg and Poppo van Henneberg.
2 CONT Hildegarde then married Thimo von Nordeck-Ruppburg. Thimo was born between 1029 and 1089, in Henneberg, Sachsen-Meiningen, Thüringen, Germany. They had one son: Heinrich von Henneberg.
2 CONT '''Personal Info'''
2 CONT Her occupation was gravin van Henneberg en Nordeck.
2 CONT '''Death'''
2 CONT Hildegarde passed away in 1104, at age 45 in Henneberg, Henneberg, Deutschland(HRR)
2 CONT Source: “Hildegarde van Schauenburg Family History & Historical Records - MyHeritage.” MyHeritage, 2019, www.myheritage.com/names/hildegarde_van%20schauenburg. Accessed 11 Oct. 2025.  (Appears to be an AI summary of individual tre
2 CONC es)
