1 OCCU Count of Northeim, Count in Rittergau, Duke of Bavaria 1061-1070, Graf von Northeim, Herzog in Bayern, Hertug af Bayern 1061-1070, Herzog von Bayern, Graf von Northeim und Bomeneburg
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Otto von Northeim''' (c1020 - 11 January 1083), Count of Northeim, Count in Rittegau, Vogt of Cowey, and Duke of Bavaria (1061-1070). He was made Duke of Bavaria by the Empress Agnes, but removed in 1070 for reb
2 CONC elling against the Emperor [http://www.geni.com/people/Heinrich-IV-del-Sacro-Imperio-Romano-Germ%C3%A1nico/5020469289250100933 Henry IV].
2 CONT
2 CONT http://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Otto_von_Northeim
2 CONT
2 CONT =Otto von Northeim=
2 CONT
2 CONT aus Wikipedia, der freien Enzyklopädie
2 CONT
2 CONT *'''Otto von Northeim''' (* um 1020 ; † 11. Januar 1083) aus der Familie der Grafen von Northeim war von 1061 bis 1070 als Otto II. Herzog von Bayern. Er war der Anführer der aufständischen Sachsen im Sachsenkrieg gegen Heinrich I
2 CONC V.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Herkunft und Aufstieg==
2 CONT
2 CONT Otto wurde um 1020 als einziger Sohn des sächsischen '''Grafen Bernhard von Northeim''' und der '''Gräfin Eilika''' geboren. Er folgte als Otto I. um 1049 seinem Vater als Graf von Northeim und gehörte damit neben den Billungern u
2 CONC nd den Grafen von Stade (Udonen) zu den einflussreichsten Persönlichkeiten im östlichen Sachsen.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Ehe und Nachkommen==
2 CONT *Otto war seit etwa 1050 mit '''Richenza''' verheiratet, die in erster Ehe mit '''Graf Hermann III. von Werl''' vermählt gewesen war. Richenza wurde als Tochter '''Herzog Ottos von Schwaben''' und damit als Ezzonin vermutet, dürft
2 CONC e aber eher eine Erbtochter der billungischen Wichmann-Linie gewesen sein. 
2 CONT *Aus der Ehe gingen sieben Kinder hervor:
2 CONT # Heinrich der Fette, Markgraf von Friesland
2 CONT # Kuno, Graf von Beichlingen
2 CONT # Siegfried III., Graf von Boyneburg
2 CONT # Otto II., Graf von Northeim
2 CONT # Ida ∞ Thiemo, Graf von Brehna (Wettiner)
2 CONT # Ethelinde ∞ I Welf I. Herzog von Bayern, geschieden 1070, ∞ II Hermann, 1105-1144 bezeugt, 1115 Graf von Calvelage
2 CONT # Mathilde ∞ Graf Konrad von Arnsberg-Werl
2 CONT
2 CONT -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Otto_of_Nordheim
2 CONT
2 CONT Otto of Nordheim
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
2 CONT
2 CONT Otto of Northeim (German: Otto von Northeim), born about 1020, died 11 January 1083, was Duke of Bavaria from 1061 until 1070. He was one of the leaders of the Saxon revolt against Emperor Henry IV.
2 CONT
2 CONT Otto belonged to the rich and influential Saxon family of the counts of Northeim, and having distinguished himself in war and peace alike, in 1061 received the Stem Duchy of Bavaria from the Dowager Empress Agnes of Poitou, widow 
2 CONC of Emperor Henry III and mother of the child Emperor Henry IV. In spite of that one year later he conspired with Archbishop Anno II of Cologne to seize Henry IV at Kaiserswerth in order to deprive his mother of power. The coup suc
2 CONC ceeded, Otto led a successful expedition into Hungary in 1063 and took a prominent part in the Empire's government during the king's minority. In 1064 he went to Italy to settle a papal schism and was largely instrumental in secur
2 CONC ing the banishment from court of Archbishop Adalbert of Bremen-Hamburg. He crossed the Alps in the royal interests on two other occasions and in 1069 shared in two expeditions to the eastern Wend lands.
2 CONT
2 CONT Otto neglected his duchy, but added to his personal possessions in the southern Harz region, which ultimately led into conflict with Henry IV. In 1070 he was accused by a certain Egeno von Konradsburg of being privy to a plot to m
2 CONC urder the king, and it was decided he should submit to the ordeal of battle with his accuser at Goslar. The duke smelled treason and asked for a safe-conduct to and from the place of meeting. When this was refused he declined to a
2 CONC ppear and was consequently placed under the imperial ban and deprived of Bavaria, while his Saxon estates were plundered. He obtained no support in Bavaria, but raised an army among the Saxons and carried on a campaign of plunder 
2 CONC against Henry until 1071, when he submitted. In the following year he received back his private estates though not the ducal title.
2 CONT
2 CONT When the Saxon revolt broke out in summer 1073 Otto is represented by Bruno, the author of De bello Saxonico, as delivering an inspiring speech to the assembled Saxons at Wormsleben, after which he took command of the insurgents. 
2 CONC By the peace of Gerstungen on February 2, 1074 Bavaria was formally restored to him, which however met strong opposition with the result that Otto's former son-in-law Welf I de facto remained duke. He also participated in the seco
2 CONC nd rising of 1075 following the demolition of Harzburg Castle, after which he was again pardoned by Henry and made administrator of Saxony.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the excommunication of Henry IV by Pope Gregory VII in 1076, Otto attempted to mediate between Henry and the Saxons at Trebur, but when these efforts failed he again placed himself at their head. He assented to the election 
2 CONC of Count Rudolf of Rheinfelden as King of the Romans, when his restoration to Bavaria was assured, and by his skill and bravery inflicted defeats on Henry's forces at Mellrichstadt, Flarchheim and Hohenmolsen.
2 CONT
2 CONT Otto is described as a noble, prudent and warlike man, and he possessed great abilities. His repeated pardons showed that Henry could not afford to neglect such a powerful personality, and his military talents were repeatedly disp
2 CONC layed. He remained in arms against the king until his death on 11 January 1083. Otto is buried in the Nicolai Chapel in Northeim.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Marriage and children
2 CONT
2 CONT By his wife Richenza, probably of the Billung dynasty, widow of Count Hermann of Werl, he left four sons and three daughters.
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Henry the Fat (1055-1101), Margrave of Frisia 1055-1101
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Otto II, Count of Nordheim
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Siegfried III (1050-1107), Count of Boyneburg
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Kuno (1150/60-1103), Count of Beichlingen
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Ida, married Thimo of Wettin, was mother of Conrad, Margrave of Meissen
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Ethelinde (1160-), married Duke Welf I of Bavaria in 1062, divorced 1070; married Hermann I, Count of Calvelage in 1070
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Mathilde, married Count Konrad II of Werl-Arnsberg
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] References
2 CONT
