1 NOTE {geni:about_me} =Otto von Cappenberg=
2 CONT
2 CONT * Son of Gottfried von Cappenberg, I and Beatrix von Hildrizhausen 
2 CONT * OTTO von Cappenberg (-26 Nov 1171). The Annalista Saxo refers to the unnamed daughter of Beatrix von Schweinfurt and her husband as the wife of "Godefridus de Cappenberch", and mother of "duos filios Gotefridum et Ottonem"[2287]
2 CONC . Graf von Cappenberg. A charter dated 1125 confirms that "comites Godefridus et Otto fratres" donated property to the church of Cappenburg, witnessed by "Adolphus comes de Altena…"[2288]. A charter dated 27 Feb 1126 confirms dona
2 CONC tions to the church of Cappenburg made by "comes Godefridus et frater eius atque coheres Otto"[2289]. He and his brother transformed Cappenberg castle into a religious institution and Otto resigned his county and became a monk: "O
2 CONC tto comes quondam in castro Cappenberg et postea…in eodem loco…frater" confirmed the transformation of "castrum Cappenberg in domum religionis" by himself and "frater meus pie recordationis…dominus Godefridus" by undated charter[2
2 CONC 290]. “Otto quondam comes in Capenberg et postea prepositus eiusdem loci...” witnessed the charter dated 1170 under which Philipp Archbishop of Köln confirmed an agreement between Kloster Cappenberg and the canons of Xanten[2291].
2 CONC
2 CONT
2 CONT * [https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/116578131/otto-von_cappenberg '''Otto von Cappenberg Find A Grave Memorial''']
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/SAXON%20NOBILITY.htm#AdelheidArnsbergMAdolfIIBerg Project MedLands SAXON NOBILITY]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT '''GODEFROI, son of [HERMANN van Eenham Comte de Verdun & his [first] wife Mathilde ---] .'''  The Chronicon Hugonis names "Gregorium et Godefridum" the two sons of "Herimannus qui et Hezelo & Mathildem"[2269]. 
2 CONT same person as…?  GOTTFRIED .  Europäische Stammtafeln suggests that Graf Gottfried was the same person as the son of Hermann Comte de Verdun[2270] (see the document LOWER LOTHARINGIA NOBILITY) but the basis for this speculation i
2 CONC s not known.  Graf im Westfalen.  Graf von Cappenberg. m ---.  The name of Gottfried's wife is not known. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Graf Gottfried & his wife had one child:''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT * 1.         '''HERMANN von Cappenberg''' (-[1082/91]).  The primary source which confirms his parentage has not yet been identified.  The Vita Godefridi comitis Capenbergensis specifies that Gottfried Graf von Cappenberg "avum ha
2 CONC buit Hermannum comitem"[2271].  Graf von Cappenberg.  '''married GERBERGA von Huneburg, daughter of [GOTTFRIED von Huneburg Vogt von Neuweiler & his wife ---].'''  The Vita Godefridi comitis Capenbergensis names "Hermannum comitem
2 CONC  [et] coniuge eius Gerberge de Huneburg"[2272].  
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Graf Hermann & his wife had three children:''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT * a)         '''two sons''' (-murdered [1082/85]). The primary source which confirms their parentage has not yet been identified. 
2 CONT * b)         '''GOTTFRIED [I] von Cappenberg''' (-killed in battle 1106).  His parentage can be reconstructed from the Vita Godefridi comitis Capenbergensis which specifies that the father of Gottfried [II] Graf von Cappenberg was
2 CONC  "Godefridus" and that "avum habuit Hermannum comitem"[2273].  Graf von Cappenberg.  Otto Abbot of Werden confirmed that the abbey had acquired “curtim Hintisle contra domnum Liupponem nobilem virum” by charter dated 1092, witness
2 CONC ed by "De nobilibus comes Thiedericus de Cleue, comes Godefridus de Cappenberge et Wigboldus et Bernhardus homines eius..."[2274].  Vogt von Werden.  '''married as her first husband, BEATRIX von Hildrizhausen, daughter of HEINRICH
2 CONC  Graf von Hildrizhausen & his wife Beatrix von Schweinfurt (-[1115/22]).'''  The Annalista Saxo refers to the unnamed daughter of Beatrix von Schweinfurt and her husband as the wife of "Godefridus de Cappenberch", and mother of "d
2 CONC uos filios Gotefridum et Ottonem"[2275].  The Vita Godefridi comitis Capenbergensis names "pater eius Godefridus, mater Beatrix" as parents of Gottfried Graf von Cappenberg[2276].  The Vita Godefridi comitis Capenbergensis names "
2 CONC Heinricum comitem de Ryetbeke" as second husband of Beatrix after the death of "Godefrido"[2277].  She married secondly Heinrich Graf von Rietberg. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Graf Gottfried [I] & his wife had five children:''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT >>>>* i)          ['''ADELHEID''' .  Adelheid is shown in Europäische Stammtafeln[2278] as the daughter of Gottfried [I] Graf von Cappenberg and his wife Beatrix von Hildrizhausen.  However, another table[2279] shows Adelheid as t
2 CONC he daughter of Beatrix by her second husband Heinrich Graf von Rietberg.  The primary source which confirms her parentage and marriage has not yet been identified.  It is not therefore possible at this moment to judge which versio
2 CONC n is more likely to be correct.  '''The answer may lie in the transmisison of the advocatiam of Werden abbey, previously held by Gottfried Graf von Cappenberg and which the following charter was transmitted to Adolf [II] Graf von 
2 CONC Berg: Lüdbert Abbot of Werden and "domnum Thuringum" confirmed “precarii nostri in Dale” to Werden by charter dated 1115, witnessed by “Adolfus advocatus noster...Euerhardus frater eius, Bernherus comes...”[2280].'''  It appears p
2 CONC robable therefore that Adelheid was the daughter of Graf Gottfried [I] and that the advocatiam of Werden was her dowry.  If that is correct, Adelheid presumably married before the date of this charter.  It should be noted that Ado
2 CONC lf was recorded as Vogt of Werden in 1093 when he was still a boy (see the document LOWER RHINE NOBILITY), which suggests that arrnagements for the marriage were made when the parties were children.  '''married  ([before 1115]) as
2 CONC  his first wife, ADOLF [II] Graf von Berg, son of ADOLF [I] Graf von Berg & his wife Adelheid von Laufen (-12 Oct after 1160, bur Altenberg).]''' 
2 CONT >>>>* ii)         '''GOTTFRIED von Cappenberg''' (-27 Jan 1127).  The Vita Godefridi comitis Capenbergensis names "pater eius Godefridus, mater Beatrix" as parents of Gottfried Graf von Cappenberg, specifying also "avum habuit Her
2 CONC mannum comitem"[2281].  Graf von Cappenburg.  A charter dated 1125 confirms that "comites Godefridus et Otto fratres" donated property to the church of Cappenburg, witnessed by "Adolphus comes de Altena…"[2282].  A charter dated 2
2 CONC 7 Feb 1126 confirms donations to the church of Cappenburg made by "comes Godefridus et frater eius atque coheres Otto"[2283].  '''married  [as her first husband,] IDA [Jutta] von Arnsberg, daughter of FRIEDRICH Graf von Arnsberg [
2 CONC Werl] & his wife Adelheid van Limburg.'''  The Vita Godefridi comitis Capenbergensis refers to "Friderici comitis filiam" as the wife of Gottfried Graf von Cappenburg[2284].  The Annales Stadenses specifies that "unam filiam [Fred
2 CONC erici comitis de Arnesberch]", without naming her, was wife of "Godefridus de Cuc, pater comitem Heinrici et Frederici de Arnesburch" while "secundam filiam [Frederici comitis de Arnesberch]", without naming her either, was wife o
2 CONC f "Otto de Cappenburch, cuius filia Eilica uxor Eilmari"[2285].  Europäische Stammtafeln[2286] conflates these two possible daughters into one, married twice.  No source has been identified which confirms that this is correct, alt
2 CONC hough the death of Gottfried Graf von Cappenberg shortly before the marriage to Gottfried van Kuyc suggests that it is a possibility.  This is the solution which is shown here.  The Annales Stadenses contain many inaccuracies and 
2 CONC it is preferable not to rely exclusively on this source in the absence of independent corroboration.  This unreliability is illustrated by the reference to "Otto de Capenburch", which must be an error for Gottfried, and by the sug
2 CONC gestion that the couple were parents of Eilika, later Gräfin von Oldenburg.  The latter point contradicts the Vita Godefridi comitis Cappenbergensis which names Heinrich Graf von Rietberg (see below) as Eilika's father, which is a
2 CONC  more acceptable possibility from a chronological point of view.  Heiress of Arnsberg. ''' [She married secondly (1129) Gottfried von Malsen gt van Kuyc, who was Graf von Arnsberg from [1130].''']
2 CONT >>>>* iii)        '''OTTO von Cappenberg''' (-26 Nov 1171).  The Annalista Saxo refers to the unnamed daughter of Beatrix von Schweinfurt and her husband as the wife of "Godefridus de Cappenberch", and mother of "duos filios Gotef
2 CONC ridum et Ottonem"[2287].  Graf von Cappenberg.  A charter dated 1125 confirms that "comites Godefridus et Otto fratres" donated property to the church of Cappenburg, witnessed by "Adolphus comes de Altena…"[2288].  A charter dated
2 CONC  27 Feb 1126 confirms donations to the church of Cappenburg made by "comes Godefridus et frater eius atque coheres Otto"[2289].  He and his brother transformed Cappenberg castle into a religious institution and Otto resigned his c
2 CONC ounty and became a monk: "Otto comes quondam in castro Cappenberg et postea…in eodem loco…frater" confirmed the transformation of "castrum Cappenberg in domum religionis" by himself and "frater meus pie recordationis…dominus Godef
2 CONC ridus" by undated charter[2290].  “Otto quondam comes in Capenberg et postea prepositus eiusdem loci...” witnessed the charter dated 1170 under which Philipp Archbishop of Köln confirmed an agreement between Kloster Cappenberg and
2 CONC  the canons of Xanten[2291]. 
2 CONT >>>>* iv)       '''BEATRIX von Cappenberg''' (-29 July, bur Cappenberg, St Maria Magdalena).  The Vita Godefridi comitis Capenbergensis names "Beatricem, quæ conversa est in Capenberg et sepulta cum Eilica comitissa de Aldenburch 
2 CONC ad sancatæ Mariam Magdalenam, et Gerbergim quam clam abduxit domnus Bernherus de Erperode" as sisters of "comites Godefridus et Otto"[2292].  Nun at Cappenberg.  She founded Ilberstadt. 
2 CONT >>>>* v)        '''GERBERGA von Cappenberg .'''  The Vita Godefridi comitis Capenbergensis names "Beatricem, quæ conversa est in Capenberg et sepulta cum Eilica comitissa de Aldenburch ad sancatæ Mariam Magdalenam, et Gerbergim qu
2 CONC am clam abduxit domnus Bernherus de Erperode" as sisters of "comites Godefridus et Otto"[2293].  '''married BERNHER von Erprath,''' son of ?. 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/116578131/otto-von_cappenberg Article by Find A Grave Memorial]
2 CONT
2 CONT Otto von Cappenberg, also Odo von Cappenberg was from 1156 until his death in provost of the monastery of Cappenberg, Godfather of the Emperor Friedrich I Barbarossa. He is venerated in the Roman Catholic Church as blessed. His fe
2 CONC ast day is 23 February.
2 CONT
2 CONT Otto came from the House of Cappenberger and was the son of Gottfried I von Cappenberg, Graf im Dreingau, and his wife Beatrix von Schweinfurt, daughter of Henry II, Margrave von Schweinfurt. According to a later addition in the C
2 CONC ologne Chronicle of King he participated, together with his older brother Gottfried von Cappenberg under Lothair of Supplinburg, the later emperor Lothar III. the storming and destruction of Cathedral in the year 1121. With the co
2 CONC nquest of the city Otto and his brother burned Paul Cathedral. Since Emperor Heinrich V indicted for treason against all participants of the war train against Munster, the imposition of the ban threatened the brothers. From repent
2 CONC ance and to make amends, they bequeathed their entire possessions the Premonstratensian order. In 1123, Otto joined together with his brother Gottfried in the same order and became provost of Varlar, founded on his parental Castle
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT In the year 1156, Otto was third provost of the monastery of Cappenberg.
2 CONT During his time as Provost there he endowed richly the Church and their altars. Otto died in the February 1171 at Cappenberg and was buried there. His grave was destroyed by Protestant troops from Hesse but in 1648 during the thir
2 CONC ty years war but later restored.
2 CONT
2 CONT
