1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Dmitry Mikhaylovich of Tver (Russian: Дми́трий Миха́йлович Тверcко́й), nicknamed The Terrible Eyes (Гро́зные О́чи)
2 CONT
2 CONT Born: 1299
2 CONT
2 CONT Died: 15 September, 1326
2 CONT
2 CONT Father: Mikhail of Tver 
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother: Anna of Kashin
2 CONT
2 CONT Spouse: Maria of Lithuania
2 CONT
2 CONT Issue: None
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dmitry_of_Tver
2 CONT
2 CONT Dmitry Mikhaylovich was a Grand Prince of Vladimir (from 1322 to 1326) and Grand Prince of Tver (from 1318 to 1326). He was a son of Mikhail of Tver and Anna of Kashin.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dmitry continued his father's fight with Grand Prince Yuri Danilovich of Moscow for the yarlik (also iarlik) that is, the diploma or patent of office for the title of Grand Prince of Vladimir, which was granted by the Khan of the 
2 CONC Golden Horde. The title was much desired because the Grand Prince of Vladimir was the khan's tax-collector in Rus', and as such could gain authority and real power over the other princes of Rus'.
2 CONT
2 CONT Following Yury's machinations which led the khan to grant the yarlik to Moscow and their father's execution at the Horde in 1318, Dmitry and his brother, Alexander, fought a series of battles with Yury and intrigued against him at
2 CONC  the Horde, culminating in Dmitry's acquisition of the yarlik of office for the grand princely throne in 1322 and his murder of Yury at the Horde (in Sarai) three years later. Dmitry was himself arrested for the murder and execute
2 CONC d in Sarai on the orders of Uzbeg Khan in 1326. His remains were taken back to Tver and interred in the cathedral there.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/RUSSIA,%20Rurik.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT  DMITRY Mikhailovich "Groznye Otchi/Menacing Ees" (15 Oct 1299-executed [Sarai] 15 Sep 1325).  He succeeded his father in 1318 as Prince of Tver.  He succeeded in 1322 as DMITRY Grand Prince of Vladimir, after his father's first c
2 CONC ousin Iurii Daniilovich was deposed by the Mongols.  He murdered his predecessor, and was executed by the Mongols for this crime[889].  m (1320) MARIA of Lithuania, daughter of GEDIMINAS Grand Duke of Lithuania & his [third] wife 
2 CONC [Ievna [Eva] Ivanovna] ([1305]-1348).  She retired to a convent at Tver after her husband was killed.
