2 TITL 1854 год, Потомки Святого Благоверного Князя Михаила Черниговского по книге Петра Долгорукова.pdf
1 NOTE Ottakar of Bohemia was the successful claimant for the throne of the Empire of Austria in 1253.  He was elected by the Estates of Austria, succeeded, after several years, Herman von Baden. and prior to that, Frederick II.  Ottakar
2 CONC  was ousted by Rudolf I of Hapsburg in 1276, and was killed in an attempt to regain his kingdom.  He was succeeded by Rudolf I Duke of Hapsburg who became Holy Roman Emperor.
1 NOTE roi de Bohême de 1253 à 1278.
2 CONT
2 CONT Il est le fils de Venceslas Ier Premysl et de Cunegonde (Kunhuta en tchèque) de Hohenstaufen, ce qui fait de lui le petit fils de Philippe de Souabe. Du vivant de son père, il règne sur la Moravie (1247).
2 CONT
2 CONT Ottokar II Premysl apparaît comme le plus puissant souverain tchèque de sa dynastie. Il s’assure l’appui de la bourgeoisie des villes et de la petite noblesse des chevaliers. Il procède à une colonisation massive avec des Allemand
2 CONC s pour développer l’exploitation des mines d’argent découvertes sur le pourtour de la Bohême. 
2 CONT Il participe à une croisade avec les chevaliers teutoniques en Prusse et fonde avec eux la forteresse de Königsberg (1254). En Europe centrale, à la suite d’héritages, il annexe la Carinthie, la Carniole et la Styrie, constituant 
2 CONC un vaste empire, de la Bohême à l’Adriatique.
2 CONT
2 CONT En 1260, il vainc Etienne V de Hongrie à Kroissenbrunn. Etienne V doit céder à la Bohême la Silésie, la Slovaquie, la Lusace, l’Autriche et la Styrie (1260-1269). En 1272, il pose sa candidature au titre de « roi des Romains » mai
2 CONC s en est écarté au profit de Rodolphe Ier de Habsbourg. Une guerre s’ensuit dans laquelle Ottokar perd toutes ses conquêtes de la région des Alpes. En 1276, Rodolphe Ier et ses alliés mettent le siège devant Vienne. Le 26 novembre
2 CONC , Ottokar II de Bohême cède l’Autriche, la Styrie, la Carinthie et la Carniole à l’empereur augmentant considérablement la puissance des Habsbourg. Ottokar ne conserve que la Bohême et la Moravie.
2 CONT
2 CONT Il est vaincu et tué à Dürnkrut, dans le Marchfeld, près de Vienne par l’empereur Rodolphe Ier de Habsbourg (26 août 1278). Rodolphe met la Bohême sous sa domination et s’attribue les possessions autrichiennes du vaincu. Rodolphe 
2 CONC II, fils de l’empereur, épouse Agnès, fille d’Ottokar. Wenceslas, fils d’Ottokar, épouse Gutta, fille de Rodolphe.
2 CONT
2 CONT En 1252, il épousa Marguerite de Babenberg sœur de Frédéric le Batailleur et héritière de l'Autriche et de la Styrie. Agée de 18 ans de plus que lui, il en divorce à l'automne 1261 ne laisse aucun enfant de cette union.
2 CONT
2 CONT Par contre il eut au moins un fils illégitime (prince Mikulas Opavsky 1255-1318) avec Anezka von Kuenring(u) "Palcerik" dame de compagnie de Marguerite de Babenberg.
2 CONT
2 CONT En 1261 il épouse Cunégonde (1245-1285) fille de "Rotislav de Tchernigov et Halicz" (petite fille de Béla IV de Hongrie) d'où :
2 CONT Cunégonde (1265-1321) qui épouse le duc Boleslas II de Mazovi. 
2 CONT Agnès (1268-1296) qui épouse Rodolphe II d'Autriche 
2 CONT Venceslas (1271-1305)
1 NOTE Ottokar II (Czech: Premysl II. Otakar; c. 1233 - 26 August 1278), called The Iron and Golden King, was the King of Bohemia from 1253 until 1278. He was the Duke of Austria (1251-1276), Styria (1260-1276), Carinthia (1269-1276) and
2 CONC  Carniola (1269-1276) also.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ottokar was the second son of King Wenceslaus I of the Premyslid dynasty, and through his mother, Kunigunde of Hohenstaufen, was related to the Hohenstaufen family, being a grandson of the German king, Philip of Swabia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Rise to power
2 CONT Ottokar was originally educated for the role of an ecclesiastical administrator. However, after the death in 1247 of Vladislaus, Margrave of Moravia, Ottokar's older brother and the heir of Bohemia, Ottokar became the heir. Accord
2 CONC ing to popular oral tradition, Ottokar was profoundly shocked by his brother's death and did not involve himself in politics, becoming focused on hunting and drinking. In 1248 he was enticed by discontented nobles to lead a rebell
2 CONC ion against his father, King Wenceslaus. During this rebellion he received the nickname "the younger King" (mladší král).
2 CONT
2 CONT Wenceslaus managed to defeat the rebels and imprisoned his son.[1] Ottokar II held the title of King of Bohemia from 31 July 1248 to November, 1249.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Father and son were eventually reconciled to assist the King's aim of acquiring the neighbouring Duchy of Austria. The Duchy had been without a ruler since the death of Duke Frederick II in 1246. Wenceslaus initially attempted to 
2 CONC acquire the duchy by marrying his heir, Vladislav, to the last Duke's niece Gertrude. That match had been cut short by Vladislav's death and Gertrude's re-marriage to the Margrave of Baden. The latter was rejected by the Austrian 
2 CONC estates and could not establish his rule in Austria. Wenceslaus used this as pretext to invade Austria in 1250 - according to some sources, the estates called upon him in to restore order.
2 CONT
2 CONT Wenceslaus released Ottokar very soon and in 1251 made him Margrave of Moravia and installed him, with the approval of the Austria nobles, as governor of Austria. Ottokar entered Austria, where the estates acclaimed him as Duke. T
2 CONC o legitimize his position, Ottokar married the late Duke's (Frederick II) daughter Margaret, who was his senior by thirty years and was the widow of Henry of Hohenstaufen (who, ironically, had been engaged to Ottokar's aunt Saint 
2 CONC Agnes of Bohemia prior to marrying Margaret). Their marriage took place on 11 February 1252.[3] [4]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Ottokar in Austria 1251In 1253, King Wenceslaus died and Ottokar succeeded his father as King of Bohemia. After the death of the German King Konrad IV in 1254, Ottokar also hoped at obtaining the Imperial dignity for himself, but 
2 CONC his election bid was unsuccessful and instead Richard of Cornwall was elected.
2 CONT
2 CONT Building up of the empire
2 CONT
2 CONT Areas ruled by Ottokar IIFeeling threatened by Ottokar's growing regional power, Béla IV of Hungary, King of Hungary challenged the young King. Bela formed a loose alliance with the Duke of Bavaria and claimed the Duchy of Styria
2 CONC , which had been a component of Austria since 1192. The conflict was quelled through the Pope's mediation. It was agreed that Ottokar was to yield large parts of Styria to Bela in exchange for recognition of his right to the remai
2 CONC nder of Austria. However, after a few years the conflict resumed and Ottokar defeated the Hungarians in July 1260 at the Battle of Kressenbrunn.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bela now ceded Styria back to Ottokar, and his claim to those territories was formally recognized by Richard, Earl of Cornwall who was King of the Romans, the nominal liege lord of all German lands. This peace agreement was also s
2 CONC ealed by a royal marriage. Ottokar ended his marriage to Margaret and married Bela's young granddaughter Kunigunda of Slavonia. Kunigunde became the mother of his children, the youngest of them became his only legitimate son Wence
2 CONC slaus.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ottokar II also led two expeditions against the pagan Old Prussians and founded Königsberg, which was named in his honour and later became the capital of Prussia.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1269 he inherited Carinthia and part of Carniola. His claim was once again contested by the Hungarians on the field of battle. After another victory he became the most powerful prince within the Empire.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT The way to the final battle
2 CONT A new election for the Imperial German throne took place in 1273. But Ottokar was again not the successful candidate. He refused to recognize his victorious rival, Rudolph of Habsburg, and urged the Pope to adopt a similar policy.
2 CONC  At a convention of the Reichstag at Frankfurt in 1274, Rudolph decreed that all imperial lands that had changed hands since the death of Emperor Frederick II must be returned to the crown. This would have deprived Ottokar of Styr
2 CONC ia, Austria, and Carinthia.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1276 Rudolph placed Ottokar under the ban of the empire and besieged Vienna. This compelled Otakar in November 1276 to sign a new treaty by which he gave up all claims to Austria and the neighbouring duchies, retaining for hims
2 CONC elf only Bohemia and Moravia. Ottokar's son Wenceslaus was also betrothed to Rudolph's daughter Judith. It was an uneasy peace. Two years later, the Bohemian king tried to recover his lost lands by force. He found allies and colle
2 CONC cted a large army, but he was defeated by Rudolph and killed at the Battle of Dürnkrut and Jedenspeigen on the March on 26 August 1278.
2 CONT
2 CONT His son Wenceslaus II of Bohemia succeeded him as King of Bohemia and Margrave of Moravia.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage and children
2 CONT On 11 February 1252, Ottokar married Margaret, Duchess of Austria. Their marriage was childless and Margared was repudiated in 1261.[5] On 25 October 1261, Ottokar married his second wife Kunigunda of Slavonia. Probably they had f
2 CONC our children[6]:
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry of Bohemia (1262 - 1263)?. Died young. 
2 CONT Kunigunde of Bohemia (January, 1265 - 27 November 1321). Married Boleslaus II of Masovia. 
2 CONT Agnes of Bohemia (5 September 1269 - 17 May 1296). Married Rudolf II, Duke of Austria. 
2 CONT Wenceslaus II of Bohemia (17 September 1271 - 21 June 1305). 
2 CONT Ottokar had two natural sons and some daughters, the most important of this issue was Nicholas I, Duke of Troppau.
2 CONT
2 CONT Nicholas I, Duke of Troppau (1255-1318) ["Mikolaj I Opawski" (Przemyslida)polish translation]. Was one of king [Ottokar II of Bohemia] sons and also an ancestor of the Premyslid dynasty. The Premyslid reigned in Bohemia from the 9
2 CONC th century to 1306 and in Poland (1300-1306). King Ottokar II created the Duchy of Troppau before 1269 to provide for his son, prince Nicholas I. Today it's a part of Czech. 
2 CONT Nicolas II of Bohemia, the Duke of Troppau from 1318. [Pl "Mikolaj II Opawski"(Przemyslida)]. 
2 CONT Prince Nicholas II of Bohemia, was Nicholas I oldest son. He inherited the Duchy of Troppau after his fathers death. In 1336 Duke Nicholas II also inherited the Duchy of Ratibor, which he ruled in personal union until his death, w
2 CONC hen his eldest son Jan I succeeded him. Nicholas II of Bohemia, Duke of Troppau and Ratibor died 8 december 1365 and was probably buried in the Opawskich church.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Legacy
2 CONT
2 CONT Burial crown of Ottokar II of Bohemia at Prague CastleOttokar is considered the greatest King of Bohemia, together with Charles IV. He was a founder of many new towns (about 30-not only in Bohemia, Moravia and Silesia, but also in
2 CONC  Austria and Styria) and incorporated many existing settlements through civic charters. He was a strong proponent of trade, law and order. Furthermore, he instituted open immigration policies through which skilled German-speaking 
2 CONC immigrants settled in major cities throughout his domains.
2 CONT
2 CONT He is a famous figure both in history and in folkloric legend. In the Divine Comedy by Dante, Ottokar is seen outside the gates of Purgatory, in amiable companionship with his imperial rival Rudolph. He is also the protagonist of 
2 CONC a tragedy by the 19th-century Austrian playwright Franz Grillparzer.
