1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''He is a Direct Descendant of Charlemagne.'''
2 CONT This is his lineage:
2 CONT
2 CONT Charlemagne
2 CONT
2 CONT Louis I, The Pious
2 CONT
2 CONT Giséle of Cysoing 
2 CONT
2 CONT Berengar I, emperor of the Romans
2 CONT
2 CONT Gisela of Friuli 
2 CONT
2 CONT Berengar II of Ivrea, king of Italy 
2 CONT
2 CONT Adalbert II, king of Italy
2 CONT
2 CONT Otto Guillaume I, comte de Bourgogne et de Mâcon 
2 CONT
2 CONT Reginald I of Ivrea, count palatine of Burgundy
2 CONT
2 CONT William I "the Great" count of Burgundy
2 CONT
2 CONT Raymond of Burgundy, Count of Galicia
2 CONT
2 CONT Alfonso VII the Emperor, King of Castile and Leon
2 CONT
2 CONT Fernando II, rey de León [25th gg]
2 CONT
2 CONT Alfonso IX, king of Leon and Galicia
2 CONT
2 CONT Saint Ferdinand III, king of Castile & León
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Excellent bio and breakdown of complicated family: http://edwardthesecond.blogspot.co.uk/2009/07/spanish-warrior-saint.html
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ferdinand_III_of_Castile
2 CONT
2 CONT https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fernando_III_de_Castilla
2 CONT
2 CONT Fernando III de Castilla y de León, llamado el Santo (Peleas de Arriba, o Bolaños de Calatrava, Ciudad Real, c. 5 de agosto de 1199 – Sevilla, 30 de mayo 1252), fue Rey de Castilla (1217 – 1252) y de León (1230 – 1252). Es también
2 CONC  conocido como Santo Rey Don Fernando, que fue canonizado por la Iglesia Católica en 1671, siendo Papa Clemente X. Asimismo fue el fundador de la villa de La Rinconada.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hijo del rey Alfonso IX de León y de Berenguela de Castilla, el Papa Inocencio III declaró nulo en 1204 el matrimonio alegando el parentesco de los cónyuges, tras lo cual Berenguela volvió a la corte de su padre (el rey de Castill
2 CONC a) con todos sus hijos.
2 CONT
2 CONT En 1219 casó con Elisabeth Hohenstaufen (Beatriz de Suabia) con la que tuvo diez hijos: 
2 CONT
2 CONT  1) Alfonso, su sucesor
2 CONT
2 CONT  2) Fadrique
2 CONT
2 CONT  3) Fernando (1225–1243/1248)
2 CONT
2 CONT  4) Leonor (nacida 1227), muerta joven
2 CONT
2 CONT  5) Berenguela (1228–1288/89)
2 CONT
2 CONT  6) Enrique
2 CONT
2 CONT  7) Felipe (1231–1274)
2 CONT
2 CONT  8) Sancho, Arzobispo de Toledo y Sevilla (1233–1261)
2 CONT
2 CONT  9) Manuel, Señor de Villena
2 CONT
2 CONT 10) María, muerta en la infancia
2 CONT
2 CONT Tras quedar viudo, casa en 1237 con Juana de Danmartín y tienen cinco hijos: 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1) Fernando (1239–1269), conde de Aumale
2 CONT
2 CONT 2) Leonor, casada con Eduardo I de Inglaterra
2 CONT
2 CONT 3) Luis (1243–1269), casado con Juana Gómez de Manzanedo
2 CONT
2 CONT 4) Jimeno (1244), muerto joven y enterrado en un monasterio de Toledo
2 CONT
2 CONT 5) Juan (1245), muerto joven y enterrado en la catedral de Córdoba
2 CONT
2 CONT Tras la temprana muerte del rey de Castilla Enrique I, hermano menor de su madre y la abdicación de ésta, obtiene en 1217 el reino de Castilla, en un acto realizado en la Plaza Mayor de Valladolid. Una vez nombrado rey, tuvo que e
2 CONC nfrentarse a la casa de los Lara por una revuelta nobiliaria, fomentada por el vecino Reino de León. Contrae matrimonio con Beatriz de Suabia (1219). A partir de 1224, aprovechando las discordias surgidas entre los almohades a la 
2 CONC muerte de Abu Yacub Yusuf, dedicó su esfuerzo a dirigir las campañas de conquista de los territorios dominados por los musulmanes, combinando hábilmente las acciones diplomáticas con beneficiosas intervenciones bélicas que se valí
2 CONC an de las discordias existentes en los distintos reinos musulmanes. Así, entre 1225 y 1227 las tropas castellanas se hacen con Andújar, Martos y Baeza, lugares clave para la conquista de Andalucía.
2 CONT
2 CONT A la muerte de su padre Alfonso IX en 1230, rey de León, los partidarios de Fernando no respetaron su testamento, reivindicando el trono de León, que el rey, su padre, había legado a Sancha y Dulce, hijas de su matrimonio con Tere
2 CONC sa de Portugal. Tras una reunión entre las dos princesas, Teresa de Portugal y Berenguela de Castilla, se firma el Tratado de Valencia de Don Juan, en el que se declara la inviabilidad del testamento de Alfonso IX y el traspaso de
2 CONC  la corona de León a Fernando a cambio de una compensación económica a Dulce y Sancha, que incluía la cesión de tierras que se reincorporarían a Castilla cuando éstas murieran. De ese modo se unieron dinásticamente -siguieron cons
2 CONC ervando Cortes, leyes e instituciones diferentes- León y Castilla en la persona de Fernando.
2 CONT
2 CONT Tras lograr la unión de sus reinos, se dedica de manera sistemática a la conquista del valle del Guadalquivir. En 1231 tomó el pueblo de Cazorla en Jaén, junto al arzobispo de Toledo, Rodrigo Jiménez de Rada. Las fuerzas reales se
2 CONC  adueñan posteriormente de la campiña cordobesa y de forma inesperada se apoderan de la capital cordobesa en 1236. En 1243, el rey del taifa de Murcia se sometió a vasallaje y poco después su hijo, el Infante Alfonso, ocupó el rei
2 CONC no murciano de forma pacífica. En 1244, se establecen las fronteras con el Reino de Aragón en el Tratado de Almizra, asignando al reino de Castilla las plazas de Orihuela, Elche y Alicante.
2 CONT
2 CONT Desde entonces fue avanzando por el Guadalquivir. Jaén es conquistada tras años de ataques en 1246, y en noviembre del año 1248 se apodera de Sevilla, tras quince meses de asedio y con el auxilio del marino Ramón de Bonifaz, a qui
2 CONC en el rey había encargado en 1247 la formación de una flota con naves procedentes del Cantábrico y con la que habría de remontar el río Guadalquivir y completar el cerco sobre la ciudad. A la toma de Sevilla siguió la de Medina Si
2 CONC donia y Arcos de la Frontera, entre otras. Cuando falleció en 1252, preparaba una expedición contra el norte de África, tratando de evitar las posibles amenazas que pudieran proceder de esa zona.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ---------------------
2 CONT Canonized February 4, 1671; feast day May 30
2 CONT  - fuente: Britannica
2 CONT https://www.britannica.com/biography/Ferdinand-III-king-of-Castile-and-Leon
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Ferdinand III King of Castille (M)
2 CONT
2 CONT b. circa 1200, d. 30 May 1252, #474
2 CONT
2 CONT      Ferdinand III King of Castille married Joan (?), daughter of Simon Dammartin Count of Aumale & Ponthieu and Mary (?). Ferdinand III King of Castille was born circa 1200. He was the son of Alphonso IX King of Leon and Castile 
2 CONC and Berengia (?) Queen of Castile. Ferdinand III King of Castille died on 30 May 1252.
2 CONT
2 CONT      Child of Ferdinand III King of Castille and Joan (?):
2 CONT
2 CONT     Eleanor (?)+   b. c 1244, d. 29 Nov 1290 
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Ferdinand III of Castile
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Saint Ferdinand III 
2 CONT
2 CONT Born July 30 or August 5, 1199, monastery of Valparaíso (Peleas de Arriba, Zamora) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Died May 30, 1252, Sevilla, Spain 
2 CONT
2 CONT Venerated in Roman Catholic Church 
2 CONT
2 CONT Canonized 1271, Rome by Pope Clement X 
2 CONT
2 CONT Major shrine Cathedral of Sevilla 
2 CONT
2 CONT Feast May 30 
2 CONT
2 CONT Patronage University of Salamanca; Lucena City Cathedral of Burgos; Lucena Cathedral ; Cathedral of Sevilla; of friars (Dominican, Franciscan, Trinitarian, and Mercedarian) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Saint Ferdinand III (July 30 or August 5, 1199 – May 30, 1252), was the King of Castile from 1217 and King of León from 1230. Through his second marriage he was also Count of Aumale. He finished the work done by his maternal grand
2 CONC father Alfonso VIII and consolidated the Reconquista. In 1231, he permanently united Castile and León. He was canonized in 1671 and, in Spanish, he is Fernando el Santo or San Fernando.
2 CONT
2 CONT St Ferdinand was the son of Alfonso IX of León and Berenguela of Castile. He was born at the monastery of Valparaíso (Peleas de Arriba, Zamora) in 1198 or 1199. His parents were divorced by order of Pope Innocent III in 1204. Bere
2 CONC nguela took their children, including Ferdinand, to the court of her father. In 1217, her younger brother Henry I died and she succeeded him to the Castilian throne, but she immediately surrendered it to her son Ferdinand, for who
2 CONC m she initially acted as regent. When Alfonso died in 1230, Ferdinand also inherited León, though he had to fight for it with Alfonso's designated heirs, Sancha and Dulce, the daughters of his first wife. He thus became the first 
2 CONC sovereign of both kingdoms since the death of Alfonso VII in 1157.
2 CONT
2 CONT Early in his reign, Ferdinand had to deal with a rebellion of the House of Lara. He also established a permanent border with the Kingdom of Aragon by the Treaty of Almizra (1244).
2 CONT
2 CONT St Ferdinand spent much of his reign fighting the Moors. Through diplomacy and war, exploiting the internal dissensions in the Moorish kingdoms, he triumphed in expanding Castilian power over southern Iberian Peninsula. He capture
2 CONC d the towns of Úbeda in 1233, Córdoba in 1236, Jaén in 1246, and Seville in 1248, and occupied Murcia in 1243, thereby reconquering all Andalusia save Granada, whose king nevertheless did homage to Ferdinand. Ferdinand divided the
2 CONC  conquered territories between the Knights, the Church, and the nobility, whom he endowed with great latifundias. When he took Córdoba, he ordered the Liber Iudiciorum to be adopted and observed by its citizens, and caused it to b
2 CONC e rendered, albeit inaccurately, into Castilian.
2 CONT
2 CONT United arms of Castile and León which Ferdinand first used.The capture of Córdoba was the result of an uneven and uncoordinated process whereby parts (the Ajarquía) of the city first fell to the independent almogavars of the Sierr
2 CONC a Morena to the north, which Ferdinand had not at the time subjugated.[1] Only in 1236 did Ferdinand arrive with a royal army to take Medina, the religious and administrative centre of the city.[1] Ferdinand set up a council of pa
2 CONC rtidores to divide the conquests and between 1237 and 1244 a great deal of land was parcelled out to private individuals and members of the royal family as well as the Church. On 10 March 1241, Ferdinand established seven outposts
2 CONC  to define the boundary of the province of Córdoba.
2 CONT
2 CONT On the domestic front, he strengthened the University of Salamanca and founded the current Cathedral of Burgos. He was a patron of the newest movement in the Church: that of the friars. Whereas the Benedictines and then the Cister
2 CONC cians and Cluniacs had taken a major part in the Reconquista up til then, Ferdinand founded Dominican, Franciscan, Trinitarian, and Mercedarian houses in Andalusia, thus determining the religious future of that region. Ferdinand h
2 CONC as also been credited with sustaining the convivencia in Andalusia.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Primera Crónica General de España asserts that, on his death bed, Ferdinand commended his son "you are rich in lands and in many good vassals — more so than any other king in Christendom," probably in recognition of his expans
2 CONC ive conquests.[4] He was buried within the Cathedral of Seville by his son Alfonso X. His tomb is inscribed with four languages: Arabic, Hebrew, Latin, and an early incarnation of Castilian.[5] St Ferdinand was canonized by Pope C
2 CONC lement X in 1671. Several places named San Fernando were founded across the Spanish Empire.
2 CONT
2 CONT The symbol of his power as a king was his sword Lobera.
2 CONT
