2 CONT
2 CONT Magnus I of Sweden
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT King Magnus III of Sweden, in Swedish "Magnus Ladulås (Barnlock)", was at times in error called "Magnus I".
2 CONT
2 CONT Magnus I of Sweden, son of Nicholas (Swedish: Magnus Nilsson ; Danish: Magnus Nielsen[1]), later called Magnus the Strong (born about 1106, died June 4, 1134 in the Battle of Fotevik), was a Danish duke who ruled Gothenland in sou
2 CONC thern Sweden from 1125 to 1130. His status as ruler of Sweden was disputed in his own time, but today he is recognized as one of the Swedish monarchs.[2][3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Magnus was the son of King Niels I of Denmark and Margaret Fredkulla, the second or eldest daughter of King Inge the Elder of Sweden.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Kingship
2 CONT
2 CONT When Margaret's first cousin King Inge the Younger died in 1125, Magnus claimed the throne as the eldest grandson of Inge the Elder. Magnus was recognized by the Geats (Göterna) of Gothenland, but according to the Westrogothic law
2 CONC , had to be accepted also by the Swea, another tribe to the north of the Geats. The Swea, however, had selected Ragnvald Knaphövde. According to Saxo Grammaticus, Ragnvald had shown disrespect towards the Geats by not taking a Gea
2 CONC t hostage. As retaliation, Ragnvald was murdered by Magnus's supporters not long after.
2 CONT
2 CONT Magnus married Rikissa, daughter of Boleslaw III of Poland around 1127. In 1130, Magnus backed Boleslaw III in conquering Rügen. The Polish forces together with a Danish fleet compelled the Rani to recognize Polish rule over the i
2 CONC sland.[4] Magnus is not mentioned as King in the law of Västergötland and was probably ousted from Sweden by his successor Sverker I of Sweden around 1130.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1131, Magnus had his cousin and potential rival for the Danish throne, Canute Lavard, murdered, in order to position himself as heir presumptive to his father King Niels.[1] Though he was eventually backed by Niels, Magnus foun
2 CONC d himself in a civil war against Lavard's half-brother Eric Emune.[1] He died on June 4, 1134 during the Battle of Fotevik in Scania, where his father was also decisively defeated. Niels died later the same year.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Legacy
2 CONT
2 CONT After Magnus's death, his widow Rikissa returned to the east where she married Volodar of Minsk, a Rurikid ruler of Viking origins. She later returned to Sweden and thirdly married the man who defeated Magnus, King Sverker I.
2 CONT
2 CONT Magnus's son, Canute V, contested the Danish throne with his second cousin, Svend III. When Canute died in 1157, Magnus's legitimate descent went extinct. Canute's elder son Niels, born by his wife Helena of Sweden, died in 1180. 
2 CONC Canute's illegitimate posthumous son, Valdemar, bishop of Schleswig and Prince-Archbishop of Bremen, died in 1236 as the last descendant of king Magnus.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]References
2 CONT
2 CONT ^ a b c Bricka, Carl Frederik, Dansk Biografisk Lexikon, vol. XI [Maar - Müllner], 1897, pp.45. Available online
2 CONT
2 CONT ^ Kings and Rulers of Sweden ISBN 91-87064-35-9 pp. 3 & 15
2 CONT
2 CONT ^ Swedish Royal Court official list of monarchs
2 CONT
2 CONT ^ Edmund Kosiarz, Wojny na Bałtyku X–XIX w., Gdańsk 1978, p. 38.
2 CONT
