1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://genealogics.org/getperson.php?personID=I00218900&tree=LEO
2 CONT
2 CONT Eudoxia Dmitriyevna (Russian: Евдокия Дмитриевна)—monastic name, Euphrosyne— (? - 1407) was a Grand Duchess of Muscovy and wife of Dmitry Donskoy.
2 CONT
2 CONT Contents
2 CONT
2 CONT 1 Family 
2 CONT
2 CONT 2 Marriage 
2 CONT
2 CONT 3 Religious works 
2 CONT
2 CONT 4 Children 
2 CONT
2 CONT 5 Legacy 
2 CONT
2 CONT 6 References 
2 CONT
2 CONT 7 External links 
2 CONT
2 CONT  Family
2 CONT
2 CONT Eudoxia Dmitriyevna was a daughter of Dmitry Konstantinovich, Grand Prince of Nizhny Novgorod and Vasilisa of Rostov.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her maternal grandparents were Konstantin Vasilievich, Prince of Rostov and Maria of Moscow.
2 CONT
2 CONT Maria was a daughter of Ivan I of Moscow and his first wife Helena.
2 CONT
2 CONT  Marriage
2 CONT
2 CONT On 18 January 1367, Eudoxia married Grand Prince Dmitry Donskoy. In 1382, she stayed in Moscow in the absence of her husband, while the army of khan Tokhtamysh was approaching the capital. After the birth of her son Andrey Dmitriy
2 CONC evich, she attempted to leave Moscow, but was detained by the Muscovites, who agreed to let her go only after long negotiations.
2 CONT
2 CONT  Religious works
2 CONT
2 CONT After her husband's death, Eudoxia became known for her pious ways; the legend has it that she possessed the gift of healing. In 1393, she founded the Church of the Nativity of the Theotokos (Церковь Рождества Богородицы), the old
2 CONC est surviving building in Moscow. The church was dedicated to the Virgin's Nativity, because on this feast her husband defeated the Tatars in the Battle of Kulikovo.
2 CONT
2 CONT Four years later, Eudoxia established the Ascension Monastery next to the Frolovskaya (Spasskaya) Tower of the Moscow Kremlin. Later in her life, Eudoxia Dmitriyevna took the veil at the Ascension Monastery under the name of Yefro
2 CONC siniya (Euphrosyne) and remained there until her death in 1407. She was canonized by the Russian Orthodox Church.
2 CONT
2 CONT  Children
2 CONT
2 CONT Eudoxia and Dmitri Donskoi had at least twelve children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Daniil Dmitrievich (c. 1370 - 15 September 1379). 
2 CONT
2 CONT Vasily I of Moscow (30 September 1371 - 27 Febtuary, 1425). 
2 CONT
2 CONT Sofia Dmitrievna. Married Fyodor Olegovich, Prince of Ryazan (reigned 1402-1427). 
2 CONT
2 CONT Yury Dmitrievich, Duke of Zvenigorod and Galich (26 November 1374 - 5 June 1434). Claimed the throne of Moscow against his nephew Vasily II of Moscow. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Maria Dmitrievna (d. 15 May 1399). Married Lengvenis. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Anastasia Dmitrievna. Married Ivan Vsevolodich, Prince of Kholm]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Simeon Dmitrievich (d. 11 September 1379). 
2 CONT
2 CONT Ivan Dmitrievich (d. 1393). 
2 CONT
2 CONT Andrei Dmitrievich, Prince of Mozhaysk (14 August 1382 - 9 July 1432). 
2 CONT
2 CONT Piotr Dmitrievich, Prince of Dmitrov (29 July 1385 - 10 August 1428). 
2 CONT
2 CONT Anna Dmitrievna (born 8 January 1387). Married Yuri Patrikievich. Her husband was a son of Patrikej, Prince of Starodub and his wife Helena. His paternal grandfather was Narimantas. The marriage solidified his role as a Boyar atta
2 CONC ched to Moscow. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Konstantin Dmitrievich, Prince of Pskov (14 May/15 May 1389 - 1433). 
2 CONT
2 CONT  Legacy
2 CONT
2 CONT On 15 August 2007, the Holy Synod of the Russian Orthodox Church instituted the Order of St. Euphrosyne, named after Eudoxia, who was the first noblewoman of Moscow to enter monasticism. The award was established to commemorate th
2 CONC e 600th anniversary of Euphrosyne’s passing away. According to the synod’s ukase (decree), the new decoration will be given to women for special contributions towards the strengthening of spiritual and moral traditions in society,
2 CONC  development of the church’s social activities, maintaining relations between church and state or church and society, and other fields of work for the betterment of the Orthodox faith. The Order of St. Euphrosyne will be the secon
2 CONC d women’s decoration of the Russian Orthodox Church after the Order of St. Olga.[1]
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Eudoxia of Moscow
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Eudoxia Dmitriyevna (Russian: Евдокия Дмитриевна)—monastic name, Euphrosyne— (? - 1407) was a Grand Duchess of Muscovy and wife of Dmitry Donskoy.
2 CONT
2 CONT Family
2 CONT
2 CONT Eudoxia Dmitriyevna was a daughter of Dmitry Konstantinovich, Grand Prince of Nizhny Novgorod and Vasilisa of Rostov.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her maternal grandparents were Konstantin Vasilievich, Prince of Rostov and Maria of Moscow.
2 CONT
2 CONT Maria was a daughter of Ivan I of Moscow and his first wife Helena.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Marriage
2 CONT
2 CONT On 18 January 1367, Eudoxia married Grand Prince Dmitry Donskoy. In 1382, she stayed in Moscow in the absence of her husband, while the army of khan Tokhtamysh was approaching the capital. After the birth of her son Andrey Dmitriy
2 CONC evich, she attempted to leave Moscow, but was detained by the Muscovites, who agreed to let her go only after long negotiations.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Religious works
2 CONT
2 CONT After her husband's death, Eudoxia became known for her pious ways; the legend has it that she possessed the gift of healing. In 1393, she founded the Church of the Nativity of the Theotokos (Церковь Рождества Богородицы), the old
2 CONC est surviving building in Moscow. The church was dedicated to the Virgin's Nativity, because on this feast her husband defeated the Tatars in the Battle of Kulikovo.
2 CONT
2 CONT Four years later, Eudoxia established the Ascension Monastery next to the Frolovskaya (Spasskaya) Tower of the Moscow Kremlin. Later in her life, Eudoxia Dmitriyevna took the veil at the Ascension Monastery under the name of Yefro
2 CONC siniya (Euphrosyne) and remained there until her death in 1407. She was canonized by the Russian Orthodox Church.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Children
2 CONT
2 CONT Eudoxia and Dmitri Donskoi had at least twelve children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Daniil Dmitrievich (c. 1370 - 15 September 1379).
2 CONT
2 CONT Vasily I of Moscow (30 September 1371 - 27 February, 1425).
2 CONT
2 CONT Sofia Dmitrievna. Married Fyodor Olegovich, Prince of Ryazan (reigned 1402-1427).
2 CONT
2 CONT Yury Dmitrievich, Duke of Zvenigorod and Galich (26 November 1374 - 5 June 1434). Claimed the throne of Moscow against his nephew Vasily II of Moscow.
2 CONT
2 CONT Maria Dmitrievna (d. 15 May 1399). Married Lengvenis.
2 CONT
2 CONT Anastasia Dmitrievna. Married Ivan Vsevolodich, Prince of Kholm.
2 CONT
2 CONT Simeon Dmitrievich (d. 11 September 1379).
2 CONT
2 CONT Ivan Dmitrievich (d. 1393).
2 CONT
2 CONT Andrei Dmitrievich, Prince of Mozhaysk (14 August 1382 - 9 July 1432).
2 CONT
2 CONT Piotr Dmitrievich, Prince of Dmitrov (29 July 1385 - 10 August 1428).
2 CONT
2 CONT Anna Dmitrievna (born 8 January 1387). Married Yuri Patrikievich. Her husband was a son of Patrikej, Prince of Starodub and his wife Helena. His paternal grandfather was Narimantas. The marriage solidified his role as a Boyar atta
2 CONC ched to Moscow.
2 CONT
2 CONT Konstantin Dmitrievich, Prince of Pskov (14 May/15 May 1389 - 1433).
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Legacy
2 CONT
2 CONT On 15 August 2007, the Holy Synod of the Russian Orthodox Church instituted the Order of St. Euphrosyne, named after Eudoxia, who was the first noblewoman of Moscow to enter monasticism. The award was established to commemorate th
2 CONC e 600th anniversary of Euphrosyne’s passing away. According to the synod’s ukase (decree), the new decoration will be given to women for special contributions towards the strengthening of spiritual and moral traditions in society,
2 CONC  development of the church’s social activities, maintaining relations between church and state or church and society, and other fields of work for the betterment of the Orthodox faith. The Order of St. Euphrosyne will be the secon
2 CONC d women’s decoration of the Russian Orthodox Church after the Order of St. Olga.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]References
2 CONT
