1 NOTE He succeeded his grandfather, Håkon V, as king of Norway and was elected 
2 CONT king of Sweden by the Swedish nobles. His realms were administered by 
2 CONT regents from 1319 to 1332, a period in which the nobles acquired great 
2 CONT power. In 1355 he handed Norway over to his son, Håkon VI, for whom he had 
2 CONT acted as regent since 1343. In Sweden another son deposed Magnus in 1356. 
2 CONT Magnus, however, regained the crown in 1359, only to be deposed again in 
2 CONT 1363 by the nobles after he had surrendered the southern section of Sweden 
2 CONT to Denmark. Håkon VI waged war in support of his father, but Magnus did 
2 CONT not again gain control of his realm. Magnus was imprisoned for some time 
2 CONT but escaped in 1371 to Norway, where he lived until his death.
