2 CONT
2 CONT Albert II, Duke of Austria
2 CONT
2 CONT Albert II of Austria (December 12, 1298 – August 16, 1358, known as the Wise or the Lame) was Duke of Austria.
2 CONT
2 CONT Albert II was born at Habsburg, the son of Albert I of Germany, Rex Romanorum, and Elisabeth of Tirol. He became the joint ruler of all Habsburg lands with his younger brother, Otto the Merry in 1330, while increasing his possessi
2 CONC ons by the inheritance of his wife Joan, which was made up of the County of Pfirt and several cities.
2 CONT
2 CONT Furthermore, Albert succeeded in establishing his claims on Carinthia and Carniola against John of Bohemia. Reflecting his high reputation among the secular and Church leaders of Europe, in 1335 Pope Benedict XII asked him to medi
2 CONC ate in the church's conflict with Emperor Louis the Bavarian. Two years later, King Philip VI of France 1337 asked him for help against Emperor Louis and King Edward III of England. Nevertheless, Albert remained faithful to the Em
2 CONC peror until Louis' death.
2 CONT
2 CONT He established the "Albertinian House Rule" (Albertinische Hausordnung) to predetermine the rules of succession in the Austrian lands, although the rule was disregarded after his death until renewed by Maximilian I, Holy Roman Emp
2 CONC eror. Adopted as part of the Pragmatic Sanction, the Albertinian House Rule effectively remained one of the basic laws of Austria until 1918. Styria owes him its (former) constitution, the so-called "Mountain Book" (Bergbüchel); t
2 CONC he same is true for Carinthia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Albert began the construction of the Gothic Choir in St. Stephen's Cathedral in Vienna, known as the Albertinian Choir. It has been speculated that he had temporal paralysis (explaining his nickname "Albert the Lame") caused by po
2 CONC lyarthritis. If so, however, it did not prevent him from fathering four children: Rudolf, who succeeded him as duke, Frederick (2nd Duke), Albert III (3rd Duke), and Leopold (3rd co-Duke).
2 CONT
2 CONT Albert died at Vienna in 1358.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Albert II, Duke of Austria
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Albert II of Austria (December 12, 1298 – August 16, 1358, known as the Wise or the Lame) was Duke of Austria.
2 CONT
2 CONT Life
2 CONT
2 CONT Albert II was born at Habsburg, the son of Albert I of Germany, Rex Romanorum, and Elisabeth of Tirol. He became the joint ruler of all Habsburg lands with his younger brother, Otto the Merry in 1330, while increasing his possessi
2 CONC ons by the inheritance of his wife Joan, which was made up of the County of Pfirt and several cities.
2 CONT
2 CONT Furthermore, Albert succeeded in establishing his claims on Carinthia and Carniola against John of Bohemia. Reflecting his high reputation among the secular and Church leaders of Europe, in 1335 Pope Benedict XII asked him to medi
2 CONC ate in the church's conflict with Emperor Louis the Bavarian. Two years later, King Philip VI of France 1337 asked him for help against Emperor Louis and King Edward III of England. Nevertheless, Albert remained faithful to the Em
2 CONC peror until Louis' death.
2 CONT
2 CONT He established the "Albertinian House Rule" (Albertinische Hausordnung) to predetermine the rules of succession in the Austrian lands, although the rule was disregarded after his death until renewed by Maximilian I, Holy Roman Emp
2 CONC eror. Adopted as part of the Pragmatic Sanction, the Albertinian House Rule effectively remained one of the basic laws of Austria until 1918. Styria owes him its (former) constitution, the so-called "Mountain Book" (Bergbüchel); t
2 CONC he same is true for Carinthia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Albert began the construction of the Gothic Choir in St. Stephen's Cathedral in Vienna, known as the Albertinian Choir. It has been speculated that he had temporal paralysis (explaining his nickname "Albert the Lame") caused by po
2 CONC lyarthritis. If so, however, it did not prevent him from fathering four children: Rudolf, who succeeded him as duke, Frederick (2nd Duke), Albert III (3rd Duke), and Leopold (3rd co-Duke).
2 CONT
2 CONT Albert died at Vienna in 1358 and was buried in a monastery of his own foundation, Gaming Charterhouse in Lower Austria.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Family and children
2 CONT
2 CONT He married 15 February 1324 Countess Johanna of Pfirt, daughter of Count Ulrich III of Pfirt and had the following children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Rudolf IV of Austria November 1, 1339, Vienna – July 27, 1365, Milan). Married but line extinct.
2 CONT
2 CONT Frederick III of Austria (1347, Vienna – 1362, Vienna). Died unmarried.
2 CONT
2 CONT Albert III of Austria (September 9, 1349, Vienna – August 29, 1395, Castle Laxenburg).
2 CONT
2 CONT Leopold III (November 1, 1351, Vienna – July 9, 1386, Sempach).
2 CONT
2 CONT Katharina (1342, Vienna – 10 January 1381, Vienna), Abbess of St. Klara in Vienna.
2 CONT
2 CONT Margarete (1346, Vienna – 14 January 1366, Brno), married:
2 CONT
2 CONT in Passau 4 September 1359 Count Meinhard III of Gorizia-Tyrol;
2 CONT
2 CONT in Vienna 1364 Margrave Johann Heinrich of Moravia.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Notes
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]References
2 CONT
2 CONT Gabrielle Caerr-Stamm: Johanna von Pfirt, Gattin des Habsburgers Albrecht II. Herzog von Österreich oder das europäische Schicksal einer Elsässerin ("wife of Habsburg Albert II Duke of Austria or the European Fate of an Alsatian")
2 CONC . Sundgaugeschichtsverein, Riedisheim 1996, ISBN 2-908498-06-5.
2 CONT
2 CONT Franz Kurz: Österreich unter Herzog Albrecht dem Lahmen ("Austria under Duke Albert the Lame"), Haslinger, Linz 1819.
