0 @N6000000002187834277@ NOTE Philip of Swabia
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Philip of Swabia depicted in a medieval manuscript (about 1200).Philip of Swabia (1177 – June 21, 1208) was king of Germany and duke of Swabia, the rival of the emperor Otto IV.
1 CONT
1 CONT Contents [hide]
1 CONT 1 Biography 
1 CONT 2 Ancestors 
1 CONT 3 References 
1 CONT 4 Philip's descendants 
1 CONT 5 See also 
1 CONT 6 External links 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Biography
1 CONT Philip was the fifth and youngest son of the emperor Frederick I and Beatrix, daughter of Renaud III, count of Burgundy, and brother of the emperor Henry VI. He entered the clergy, was made provost of Aix-la-Chapelle, and in 1190 
1 CONC or 1191 was chosen bishop of Würzburg. Having accompanied his brother Henry to Italy in 1191, Philip forsook his ecclesiastical calling, and, travelling again to Italy, was made duke of Tuscany in 1195 and received an extensive gr
1 CONC ant of lands. In 1196 he became duke of Swabia, on the death of his brother Conrad; and in May 1197 he married Irene Angelina, daughter of the Byzantine emperor, Isaac II, and widow of Roger III, Titular King of Sicily, a lady who
1 CONC  is described by Walther von der Vogelweide as " the rose without a thorn, the dove without guile."
1 CONT
1 CONT Philip enjoyed his brother's confidence to a very great extent, and appears to have been designated as guardian of the Henry's young son Frederick, afterwards the emperor Frederick II, in case of his father's early death. In 1197 
1 CONC he had set out to fetch Frederick from Sicily for his coronation as King of the Germans when he heard of the emperor's death and returned at once to Germany. He appears to have desired to protect the interests of his nephew and to
1 CONC  quell the disorder which arose on Henry's death, but was overtaken by events. The hostility to the kingship of a child was growing, and after Philip had been chosen as defender of the empire during Frederick's minority he consent
1 CONC ed to his own election. He was elected German king at Mühlhausen on March 8, 1198, and was crowned at Mainz on the September 8 following.
1 CONT
1 CONT Meanwhile, a number of princes hostile to Philip, under the leadership of Adolph, Archbishop of Cologne, had elected an anti-king in the person of Otto, second son of Henry the Lion, duke of Saxony. In the war that followed, Phili
1 CONC p, who drew his principal support from south Germany, met with considerable success. In 1199 he received further accessions to his party and carried the war into his opponent's territory, although unable to obtain the support of P
1 CONC ope Innocent III, and only feebly assisted by his ally Philip Augustus, king of France. The following year was less favourable to his arms; and in March 1201 Innocent took the decisive step of placing Philip and his associates und
1 CONC er the ban, and began to work energetically in favour of Otto.
1 CONT
1 CONT Also in 1201, Philip was visited by his cousin Boniface of Montferrat, the leader of the Fourth Crusade. The Crusaders were by this time under Venetian control and were besieging Zara on the Adriatic Sea. Although Boniface's exact
1 CONC  reasons for meeting with Philip are unknown, while at Philip's court he also met Alexius Angelus, Philip's brother-in-law. Alexius convinced Boniface, and later the Venetians, to divert the Crusade to Constantinople and restore I
1 CONC saac II to the throne, as he had recently been deposed by Alexius III, Alexius and Irene's uncle.
1 CONT
1 CONT The two succeeding years were still more unfavourable to Philip. Otto, aided by Ottokar I, king of Bohemia, and Hermann I, landgrave of Thuringia, drove him from north Germany, thus compelling him to seek by abject concessions, bu
1 CONC t without success, reconciliation with Innocent. The submission to Philip of Hermann of Thuringia in 1204 marks the turning-point of his fortunes, and he was soon joined by Adolph of Cologne and Henry I, Duke of Brabant.
1 CONT
1 CONT On January 6, 1205 he was crowned again with great ceremony by Adolph at Aix-la-Chapelle, though it was not till 1207 that his entry into Cologne practically brought the war to a close. A month or two later Philip was loosed from 
1 CONC the papal ban, and in March 1208 it seems probable that a treaty was concluded by which a nephew of the pope was to marry one of Philip's daughters and to receive the disputed dukedom of Tuscany. Philip was preparing to crush the 
1 CONC last flicker of the rebellion in Brunswick-Lüneburg when he was murdered at Bamberg, on June 21, 1208, by Otto of Wittelsbach, count palatine in Bavaria, to whom he had refused the hand of one of his daughters. Philip was a brave 
1 CONC and handsome man, and contemporary writers, among whom was Walther von der Vogelweide, praise his mildness and generosity.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Ancestors
1 CONT Philip's ancestors in three generations Philip of Swabia Father:
1 CONT Frederick I, Holy Roman Emperor Paternal Grandfather:
1 CONT Frederick II, Duke of Swabia Paternal Great-grandfather:
1 CONT Frederick I, Duke of Swabia 
1 CONT Paternal Great-grandmother:
1 CONT Agnes of Germany 
1 CONT Paternal Grandmother:
1 CONT Judith Paternal Great-grandfather:
1 CONT Henry IX, Duke of Bavaria 
1 CONT Paternal Great-grandmother:
1 CONT Wulfhild of Saxony 
1 CONT Mother:
1 CONT Beatrice I, Countess of Burgundy Maternal Grandfather:
1 CONT Renaud III, Count of Burgundy Maternal Great-grandfather:
1 CONT Stephen I, Count of Burgundy 
1 CONT Maternal Great-grandmother:
1 CONT Beatrix of Lorraine 
1 CONT Maternal Grandmother:
1 CONT Agatha Maternal Great-grandfather:
1 CONT Simon I, Duke of Lorraine 
1 CONT Maternal Great-grandmother:
1 CONT Adelaide 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT Peter Csendes, Philipp von Schwaben. Ein Staufer im Kampf um die Macht, 2003. 
1 CONT This article incorporates text from the Encyclopædia Britannica Eleventh Edition article "Philip", a publication now in the public domain. 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Philip's descendants
1 CONT Philip of Swabia married Irene Angelina, daughter of Isaac II Angelus on May 25, 1197. Their four daughters were:
1 CONT
1 CONT Beatrice of Hohenstaufen (1198-1212), married Otto IV, Holy Roman Emper
1 CONT Cunigunde of Hohenstaufen (1200-1248), married King Wenceslaus I, King of Bohemia 
1 CONT Mary of Hohenstaufen (1201-1235), married Henry II, Duke of Brabant 
1 CONT Elisabeth of Hohenstaufen (1203-1235), married King Ferdinand III of Castile 
