0 @N6000000002188021585@ NOTE Ludwig IV of Thuringia
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Louis of Thuringia (German: Ludwig IV., der Heilige (28 October 1200, Creuzburg – 11 September 1227, Otranto), Landgrave of Thuringia in 1217-1227.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was a son of Landgrave Hermann, and Duchess Sophia, daughter of Otto of Wittelsbach, Duke of Bavaria. Ludwig ascended to the Thuringian throne at the age of 16 upon his father's death in 1216. In 1218, at age 18, on the Feast o
1 CONC f St. Kilian, he was armed as a knight in the Church of St. George in Eisenach.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1220 Louis was married at age 20 to 13-year-old Elisabeth of Hungary at the Wartburg, and they soon had two children, Hermann II and Sophie of Thuringia. He set up court in Eisenach.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1227, Louis was inspired to go on Crusade by the tales of his uncle, who had been to the Levant with the Holy Roman Emperor. He embarked on a journey to the Holy Land with five counts, Louis de Wartburg, Gunther de Kefernberg, 
1 CONC Meinrad de Muhlberg, Henry de Stolberg and Burkhard de Brandenberg, leaving his pregnant wife behind. She had a premonition that they would never meet again.
1 CONT
1 CONT In August of 1227 Louis transversed the mountains between Thuringia and Upper Franconia, through Swabia and Bavaria, crossing the Tyrolian Alps. He fell ill of a fever after reaching Brindisi and Otranto. He received Extreme Uncti
1 CONC on from the Patriarch of Jerusalem and the Bishop of Sante Croce. He died on September 11, 1227. A few days after his death, his daughter Gertrud was born.
1 CONT
1 CONT Louis' wife Elisabeth would also die young only a few years later, at the age of 24, after establishing the Benedictine Abbey of Saint Catherine of Alexandria in Eisenach. She was officially proclaimed a saint only four years afte
1 CONC r her death. While Ludwig was never formally canonized, he became known among the German people as "Ludwig der Heilige" (St. Ludwig).
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Family and children
1 CONT
1 CONT He and Elisabeth of Hungary had the following children:
1 CONT
1 CONT    1. Hermann II, Landgrave of Thuringia (1222 –1241/42).
1 CONT    2. Sophie of Thuringia (30 March 1224 – 29 May 1275).
1 CONT    3. Gertrud (1227 – 1297), Abbess at Altenberg near Wetzlar.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Hermann I Landgrave of Thuringia
1 CONT 1217–1227 Succeeded by
1 CONT Hermann II
