1 CONT Count of Gorizia Succeeded by:
1 CONT Albert II 
0 @N6000000006127668663@ NOTE Meinhard II. (* um 1238; † 1. November 1295 in Greifenburg) aus dem Geschlecht der Meinhardiner war Graf (als Meinhard IV.) von Görz und Tirol und Herzog von Kärnten (1286-1295). Er war einer der bedeutendsten Fürsten des 13. Jahr
1 CONC hunderts und derBegründer Tirols als eigenständiges Land.Als Sohn des Grafen Meinhard I. fiel ihm durch Erbteilung 1271 die Grafschaft Tirol zu. Sein Bruder Albert I. erhielt die Besitzungen in Friaul, Istrien, Kärnten und im Pust
1 CONC ertal.Berühmt wurde er durch die Prägung des „Zwainzigers“, der ersten deutschen Silbergroßmünze, die vielfach nachgeahmt wurde. Er unterstützte Rudolf I. von Habsburg in der Auseinandersetzung mit König Ottokar II. von Böhmen und
1 CONC  wurde dafür 1286 zumReichsfürsten erhoben und mit dem Herzogtum Kärnten belehnt.Gemeinsam mit seiner Gemahlin Elisabeth von Bayern, der Witwe des Staufers Konrad IV., stiftete er 1284 das Zisterzienserkloster Stams in Tirol, wo e
1 CONC r auch sein Grab fand. Seine Tochter Elisabeth heiratete Albrecht I., den deutschen König1298-1308, und wurde zur Stammmutter aller späteren Habsburger. Durch seine Unterwerfung der Fürstbistümer Trient und Brixen gilt er als der 
1 CONC eigentliche Begründer des Landes Tirol.
0 @N6000000002187700635@ NOTE Elisabeth of Tirol
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Elisabeth de Gorizia de Tirol de Carantania (c. 1262 – 28 October 1312) was Queen consort of the Holy Roman Empire and Duchess of Austria, etc.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Family
1 CONT
1 CONT She was the eldest daughter of Meinhard II, Duke of Carinthia, count of Gorizia and Tyrol.
1 CONT
1 CONT Her mother was his wife Elisabeth of Bavaria. She was a daughter of Otto II Wittelsbach, Duke of Bavaria and his wife Agnes, herself daughter of Henry V, Count Palatine of the Rhine and Agnes of Hohenstaufen. Her mother was also t
1 CONC he widow of Conrad IV of Germany. Therefore, the young Elisabeth was a half-sister of Conradin, King of Jerusalem etc and Duke of Swabia who was killed in 1268.
1 CONT
1 CONT The younger Elisabeth was from 1274 the wife of the future Albert I of Germany, one of the founders of the House of Habsburg, becoming thus daughter-in-law of the King of the Romans. Her husband, then a Count of Habsburg was inves
1 CONC ted as Duke of Austria and Styria in December 1282 by his father the King Rudolf. They solidified their rule in what was to become the Habsburg patrimony, also by help of Elisabeth's father who in his turn in 1286 was created Duke
1 CONC  of Carinthia.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1298, her husband was finally elected the King upon the end of the reign of Adolf of Nassau. Her husband was murdered on 1 May 1308 by his nephew John "the Parricide".
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Marriage and children
1 CONT
1 CONT She was married in Vienna on 20 December 1274 to the future Albert I of Germany. Their children were:
1 CONT
1 CONT    1. Rudolph III (ca. 1282–4 July 1307, Horazdiowitz), Married but line extinct and predeceased his father.
1 CONT    2. Frederick I (1289–13 January 1330, Gutenstein). Married but line extinct.
1 CONT    3. Leopold I (4 August 1290–28 February 1326, Strassburg). Married but line extinct.
1 CONT    4. Albert II (12 December 1298, Vienna–20 July 1358, Vienna).
1 CONT    5. Heinrich (1299–3 February 1327, Bruck an der Mur). Married but line extinct.
1 CONT    6. Meinhard, 1300 died young.
1 CONT    7. Otto (23 July 1301, Vienna–26 February 1339, Vienna). Married but line extinct.
1 CONT    8. Anna 1280?, Vienna–19 March 1327, Breslau), married:
1 CONT          1. in Graz ca. 1295 to Margrave Hermann of Brandenburg;
1 CONT          2. in Breslau 1310 to Duke Heinrich VI of Breslau.
1 CONT    9. Agnes (18 May 1281–10 June 1364, Königsfelden), married in Vienna 13 February 1296 King Andrew III of Hungary.
1 CONT   10. Elisabeth (d. 19 May 1353), married 1304 Frederick IV, Duke of Lorraine.
1 CONT   11. Katharina (1295–18 January 1323, Naples), married 1316 Charles, Duke of Calabria.
1 CONT   12. Jutta (d. 1329), married in Baden 26 March 1319 Count Ludwig VI of Öttingen.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Imagina of Isenburg-Limburg German Queen
1 CONT 1298–1 May 1308 Succeeded by
1 CONT Margaret of Brabant
