0 @N6000000002187798185@ NOTE Henry, Duke of Lower Lorraine
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry I (c.1059 – c.1119) was the count of Limburg and Arlon from 1082 to his death and duke of Lower Lorraine between 1101 to 1106. He was the son of Waleran I of Limburg and Jutta, daughter of Frederick, Duke of Lower Lorraine.
1 CONT
1 CONT He opposed Egilbert, Archbishop of Trier, and took back some property which the former Countess Adela had given to the church. Egilbert summoned him to return them, but he refused and was excommunicated. Egilbert took up arms and 
1 CONC seriously defeated him.
1 CONT
1 CONT As advocate of the abbey of Saint-Trond, a title he had inherited from his father, he intervened in the internal affairs of the abbey. The abbot Herman, named by Poppo, Bishop of Metz, and supported by Godfrey of Bouillon and Henr
1 CONC y, fell out with the Emperor Henry IV and the abbey was transferred to the authority of Arnold, Count of Looz. Arnold forced Henry and Godfrey to withdraw from the monastery.
1 CONT
1 CONT After many local nobles left on the First Crusade, among them Godfrey, Henry's power in the region of Belgia was greatly increased and he abused it, especially against the monasteries. The emperor intervened and took Limburg in Ju
1 CONC ly 1101. Henry was now forced to make submission and he was granted the duchy of Lower Lorraine, which Godfrey had abandoned on Crusade.
1 CONT
1 CONT As duke, he fell into competition with Godfrey I of Louvain. He demonstrated little in the way of loyalty the emperor either. He joined Henry V against his father the emperor, but then turned back to the emperor's side. This was u
1 CONC nfortunate for the duke, for the emperor died in 1106 and the partisans of Henry V attacked those of his father. The fields of Belgia were devastated, Limburg was taken, and Henry was imprisoned in Hildesheim. The duchy was transf
1 CONC erred to Godfrey of Louvain.
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry later escaped and tried to retake Limburg and Lower Lorrain. He failed and made peace with the new emperor and duke. He continued nevertheless to employ the ducal title as "Duke of Limburg," the first of a long line. He also
1 CONC  readily joined revolts against Henry V, fighting at the side of Lothair, Duke of Saxony, at the victories of Andernach in 1114 and Welphesholt on 11 February 1115. He was succeeded by his son Waleran.
1 CONT
1 CONT He married Adelaide of Podenstein (or Pottenstein) (1061 – 1106), daughter of Boso of Podenstein and Judith of Schweinfurt, daughter of Otto III, Duke of Swabia. They had the aforementioned Waleran and three daughters. He may have
1 CONC  been the father of Simon, Constable of Jerusalem after the First Crusade.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Godfrey V Duke of Lower Lorraine
1 CONT 1101 – 1106 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Godfrey VI 
