0 @N6000000002187798205@ NOTE Waleran, Duke of Lower Lorraine
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Waleran II or Walram II (c.1085 – 1139), called Paganus meaning "the Pagan", probably due to a late baptism. He was the duke of Limburg and count of Arlon from his father's death in about 1119. He was given the duchy of Lower Lorr
1 CONC aine after Lothair of Supplinburg's accession in 1125.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was the son of Henry, Duke of Lower Lorraine (1056 – 1106), and Adelaide of Pottenstein. Henry had been forced to yield the duchy to Godfrey I of Louvain on Henry V's succession, but had kept the ducal title. With the coming of
1 CONC  Lothair, Godfrey was forced to yield it to Waleran. Godfrey was not willing to do so and war broke out, especially over disputes about authority over the abbey of Saint-Trond. In 1129, Waleran and the bishop of Liège, Alexandre d
1 CONC e Juliers, demolished Godfrey's forces at Wilde. His rule was actual from there on. Though Waleran and Godfrey eventually reconciled, Godfrey maintained, as Henry had, the ducal title.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1129, Waleran was made forester of Duisbourg. In 1139, Lothair died and Waleran supported Conrad of Hohenstaufen, who was elected. He remained faithful to the new king until his death shortly thereafter. He was succeeded by God
1 CONC frey II of Louvain in Lorraine.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Godfrey VI Duke of Lower Lorraine
1 CONT 1125 – 1139 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Godfrey VII 
