0 @N6000000002187700613@ NOTE Rudolph I of Bohemia
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT Rudolf I of Habsburg (Czech: Rudolf I. Habsburský; 1281 – 3/4 July 1307, Horaždovice in Bohemia) was a king of Bohemia (1306-1307) and duke of Austria (as Rudolph III).
1 CONT
1 CONT The son of Albert I, King of the Romans, Rudolf was in 1306 presented as a rival claimant to the Bohemian throne against Henry of Carinthia. Albert had Rudolf married to Elisabeth Richeza of Poland, widow of Wenceslas II of Bohemi
1 CONC a (1278-1305) and in 1306 occupied Prague to place Rudolf on the Czech throne.
1 CONT
1 CONT As some Czech nobles continued to hold out for Henry of Carinthia, Rudolf besieged the rebel fortress of Horaždovice, but got ill of dysentery and died there in 1307. The Czech nobles then restored Henry of Carinthia as king in re
1 CONC turn for a charter of privileges.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Henry of Carinthia King of Bohemia Succeeded by:
1 CONT Henry of Carinthia 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Rudolph II Duke of Austria
1 CONT Co-ruler to Albert I
1 CONT 1298–1307 Succeeded by:
1 CONT — 
0 @N6000000002322312619@ NOTE Duke of Austria as Frederick III from 1308. After his father's murder in 1308,
1 CONT Frederick did not succeed him as king, the count of Luxembourg being
1 CONT elected instead, as Henry VII. Upon the death of Henry in 1313, two kings
1 CONT of the Germans were mistakenly elected by different archbishops in different
1 CONT places causing war between the rivals which lasted for eight years, causing
1 CONT calamity lasting beyond September 1325, when Louis of Bavaria, King Louis IV,
1 CONT accepted Frederick as co-ruler, whose rule was limited to Austria in 1326.
0 @N6000000002187700645@ NOTE Frederick I of Austria (Habsburg)
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Frederick the Handsome (born around 1289; died January 13, 1330), from the House of Habsburg, was Duke of Austria as Frederick I and King of the Romans as Frederick (III).
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Life
1 CONT Frederick was the son of Emperor Albert I and Elisabeth of Carinthia. After the death of his elder brother Rudolf and the assassination of his father in 1308, he became the ruler of Austria on behalf of himself and his younger bro
1 CONC thers.
1 CONT
1 CONT Originally, he was a friend of his cousin, Louis the Bavarian, with whom he had been raised. However, armed conflict arose, when the tutelage over the Dukes of Lower Bavaria was entrusted to Frederick.
1 CONT
1 CONT On November 9, 1313, Frederick was beaten by Louis at Gamelsdorf and had to renounce the tutelage. After the death of Henry VII, Frederick became a candidate for the Crown of the Holy Roman Empire, but Louis was elected in October
1 CONC  1314 upon the instigation of the Archbishop of Mainz with four of the seven votes. Louis was then quickly crowned in Bonn by the Archbishop of Cologne, instead of in Aachen.
1 CONT
1 CONT After several years of bloody war, victory finally seemed to be within Frederick's grasp, who was strongly supported by his brother Leopold. However, Frederick's army was in the end completely beaten near Mühldorf on the Ampfing H
1 CONC eath on September 28, 1322, where Frederick and 1300 nobles from Austria and Salzburg were captured.
1 CONT
1 CONT Louis held Frederick captive on Castle Trausnitz in the Upper Palatinate for three years, but the persistent resistance by Frederick's brother Leopold, the retreat of the King of Bohemia from his alliance and the Pope's ban induce
1 CONC d Louis to release him in the Treaty of Trausnitz of March 13, 1325. In this agreement, Frederick finally recognized Louis as legitimate ruler and undertook to return to captivity if he would not succeed in convincing his brothers
1 CONC  to succumb to Louis.
1 CONT
1 CONT As he did not manage to overcome Leopold's obstinacy, Frederick returned to Munich as a prisoner, even though the Pope had released him from his oath. Louis, who was impressed by such nobleness, renewed the old friendship with Fre
1 CONC derick and again shared his quarters with Frederick they both agreed to rule the Empire jointly.
1 CONT
1 CONT Since the Pope and the electors strongly objected to this agreement, another Treaty was signed at Ulm on January 7, 1326, according to which Frederick should administer Germany as King of the Romans, while Louis should be crowned 
1 CONC as Holy Roman Emperor in Italy.
1 CONT
1 CONT However, after Leopold's death in 1326, Frederick withdrew from the regency of the Empire and returned to rule only Austria. He died on January 13, 1330 on Castle Gutenstein in the Wienerwald, and was buried at Mauerbach in a Mona
1 CONC stery he had founded. After the latter was closed down in 1783, his remains were brought to the Cathedral of St. Stephan in Vienna.
1 CONT
1 CONT Frederick's sons with Elisabeth (originally Isabella), the daughter of King James II of Aragon, died early. Frederick's gracious return to captivity inspired Schiller to write his poem "Deutsche Treue" (German Loyalty) and Uhland 
1 CONC to his tragedy "Ludwig der Bayer" (Louis the Bavarian).
1 CONT
1 CONT  King of Germany Co-regent to :
1 CONT Louis IV 
1 CONT preceded by
1 CONT Albert I Duke of Austria succeeded by
1 CONT Albert II 
